QB.add(31,[{"id":31001,"t":"Neurotransmitter Basics","s":"A patient is given an injection of noradrenaline. Why is the injected noradrenaline not called a neurotransmitter?","o":["It is exogenous, not made by the body","It is not released by a neuron and is therefore classed as a hormone instead","It is a monoamine, and monoamines are not neurotransmitters","It acts at adrenoceptors rather than cholinergic receptors","It cannot cross the synaptic cleft"],"e":"A neurotransmitter is an endogenous chemical, released extracellularly by a neuron, used to signal to other neurons, myocytes or endocrine cells under physiological conditions. Endogenous means “ours”; noradrenaline given as a shot is exogenous. A drug that does the same job is still not a neurotransmitter.","w":["The key issue is that it is exogenous; adrenaline from the adrenal medulla acting via blood is a hormone, but that is not why the injection is excluded.","Noradrenaline is a monoamine neurotransmitter.","Adrenoceptors are its normal receptors.","It can act on receptors once there."],"k":"Neurotransmitter must be endogenous.","rn":[["","What does exogenous mean?","What does exogenous mean?","From outside the body; e.g. an injected noradrenaline shot"],["","What does endogenous mean?","What does endogenous mean?","Made by our body"]]},{"id":31002,"t":"Neurotransmitter Basics","s":"Which is NOT one of the four features of a neurotransmitter?","o":["Always excitatory at its target","An endogenous chemical","Released extracellularly by a neuron","Signals to neurons, myocytes or endocrine cells","Acts under physiological conditions"],"e":"The four features: endogenous; released extracellularly by a neuron; used to signal to other neurons, myocytes, endocrine cells; under physiological conditions. Transmitters are not classified as excitatory/inhibitory because, apart from three, all can be both.","w":["Listed.","Listed.","Listed.","Listed."],"k":"Neurotransmitter features."},{"id":31003,"t":"Neurotransmitter Basics","s":"Why are neurotransmitters classified by chemical class rather than by function?","o":["Most can be both excitatory and inhibitory","Their functions are all identical across the body, so function cannot separate them","Their chemical class decides which neuron releases them","Functional classes are used only in the CNS","All transmitters are inhibitory"],"e":"You could classify by function (inhibitory vs excitatory), but apart from three, all can be both, so it is not useful. Chemical class is useful: members share chemistry, so a drug for one monoamine often affects other monoamine systems, especially at higher doses.","w":["Functions differ; the problem is overlap of excitatory/inhibitory.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Chemical class, as function overlaps.","rn":[["","Why are neurotransmitters not classified by function?","Why are neurotransmitters not classified by function?","Most can be both; Excitatory and inhibitory"]]},{"id":31004,"t":"Neurotransmitter Basics","s":"Dopamine, noradrenaline and adrenaline share which chemical class name?","e":"Catecholamines are synthesised on one pathway: dopamine → NA → adrenaline.","k":"Catecholamines.","a":["catecholamines","catecholamine"],"rn":[["","Which 3 monoamines are catecholamines?","Which 3 monoamines are catecholamines?","Dopamine; Noradrenaline; Adrenaline"],["","Why are dopamine, noradrenaline and adrenaline called catecholamines?","Why are dopamine, noradrenaline and adrenaline called catecholamines?","Share a catechol group"]]},{"id":31005,"t":"Neurotransmitter Basics","s":"Acetylcholine belongs to which chemical class?","o":["“Others” – an ester of acetic acid and choline","Monoamines – derived from the amino acid tyrosine like dopamine","Amino acids – like glutamate and GABA","Catecholamines","Peptides only"],"e":"Acetylcholine is in “others”, as an ester of acetic acid and choline (with peptides also listed under others). It was the first transmitter system studied and understood well.","w":["Not a monoamine.","Not an amino acid.","Not.","Peptides are separate."],"k":"ACh: ester of acetic acid and choline.","rn":[["","Acetylcholine is an ester of what?","Acetylcholine is an ester of what?","Acetic acid and choline"]]},{"id":31006,"t":"Neurotransmitter Basics","s":"Why is adrenaline shown in brackets among the monoamines?","o":["It can act as a hormone as well as a transmitter","It is synthesised only in the CNS and so rarely acts in the periphery","It is not a catecholamine","It has no receptors of its own","It is an amino acid"],"e":"Adrenaline can act as a neurotransmitter but also as a hormone, depending on where it is produced. From the adrenal glands it is released into the bloodstream (hormone, the most common situation in the ANS); in a discrete amount from some neurons it is a transmitter. Noradrenaline is mainly neuronal.","w":["It is mainly from the adrenal medulla.","It is a catecholamine.","It acts on adrenoceptors.","Not."],"k":"Adrenaline: hormone or transmitter.","rn":[["","When does adrenaline act as a hormone?","When does adrenaline act as a hormone?","Released by adrenal glands; Into the bloodstream"]]},{"id":31007,"t":"Adrenergic Transmission","s":"Why did acetylcholine-modulating drugs move to later steps of NICE guidelines?","o":["ACh is everywhere, so adverse effects are large","ACh drugs were found to have no therapeutic effects in the periphery at all","ACh is only found in the CNS","ACh cannot be modulated by drugs","ACh drugs are too expensive"],"e":"ACh is everywhere (CNS, periphery, also a neuromodulator), so the adverse effects are of high magnitude and harder to control, affecting quality of life. Once other drug classes were found, these became less used.","w":["They have effects.","Everywhere.","They can.","Not stated."],"k":"ACh drugs: big ADRs.","rn":[["","Why do antidepressants cause ANS adverse effects?","Why do antidepressants cause ANS adverse effects?","Peripheral action on ANS"]]},{"id":31008,"t":"Neurotransmitter Basics","s":"Which pairing of transmitter and receptor name is correct?","o":["Noradrenaline – adrenoceptors","Noradrenaline – noradrenoceptors, separate from adrenaline’s receptors","Acetylcholine – adrenoceptors","Adrenaline – cholinergic receptors","Dopamine – adrenoceptors"],"e":"Noradrenaline and adrenaline act on the same receptors – adrenoceptors (or adrenergic). Acetylcholine: cholinergic receptors. Dopamine: dopamine receptors. Serotonin: serotonin receptors.","w":["NA and Adr share adrenoceptors – “Same receptors!”","ACh – cholinergic.","Adrenoceptors.","Dopamine receptors."],"k":"NA and Adr: same adrenoceptors.","rn":[["","What are noradrenaline receptors called?","What are noradrenaline receptors called?","Adrenoceptors (adrenergic)"],["","Noradrenaline and adrenaline: same or different receptors?","Noradrenaline and adrenaline: same or different receptors?","Same receptors; Adrenoceptors"]]},{"id":31009,"t":"Neurotransmitter Basics","s":"What does “anticholinergic drug” mean?","o":["Any drug that decreases ACh transmission","Any drug that increases ACh transmission by stimulating cholinergic neurons","A drug that releases noradrenaline","A drug acting only on nicotinic receptors","A drug that synthesises ACh"],"e":"Cholinergic drugs modulate acetylcholine in a positive way. Anticholinergic means an antagonist, a blocker or any drug that decreases acetylcholine transmission.","w":["That is cholinergic.","Not.","Muscarinic too.","Not."],"k":"Anticholinergic = ↓ ACh.","rn":[["","What does an anticholinergic drug do?","What does an anticholinergic drug do?","Decreases acetylcholine transmission; e.g. antagonist or blocker"]]},{"id":31010,"t":"Neurotransmitter Basics","s":"Neurons synthesise multiple transmitters. How is a neuron named?","o":["After the transmitter it makes in the highest amount","After every transmitter it makes","After its receptor type","After the organ it supplies","After its spinal level"],"e":"Neurons can synthesise multiple transmitters, but they always have one in higher amounts, and that is the name they take (x-ergic: cholinergic, noradrenergic, etc.).","w":["It takes the name of the main one.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"x-ergic: main transmitter.","rn":[["","What is a multi-transmitter neurone named after?","What is a multi-transmitter neurone named after?","The one made; In highest amount"]]},{"id":31011,"t":"Neurotransmitter Basics","s":"What triggers vesicle fusion and release of a neurotransmitter?","o":["Calcium entry after depolarisation","Sodium leaving the terminal after hyperpolarisation of the presynaptic membrane","Binding of the transmitter to presynaptic α2","Reuptake by NET","Breakdown by MAO"],"e":"When the membrane is depolarised, calcium enters, and it is the calcium entry that drives vesicle fusion and release into the cleft.","w":["Calcium entry after depolarisation.","That reduces release.","Termination.","Termination."],"k":"Ca²⁺ entry → release.","rn":[["","What triggers vesicle fusion and release?","What triggers vesicle fusion and release?","Depolarisation; Calcium entry"]]},{"id":31012,"t":"Neurotransmitter Basics","s":"Which are the two ways neurotransmitter signals are terminated?","o":["Degradation, or reuptake/uptake","Degradation, or binding to more postsynaptic receptors to use the transmitter up","Vesicle storage, or synthesis","Calcium entry, or depolarisation","Diffusion into blood only"],"e":"Signal termination is by degradation (e.g. enzymes bound to the membrane in the cleft) or re-uptake by the releasing cell / uptake by neighbouring cells. Once out of the cleft the transmitter cannot act.","w":["Binding doesn’t terminate.","Earlier steps.","Release triggers.","Not."],"k":"Termination: degradation or reuptake.","rn":[["","What are the 2 ways a signal is terminated?","What are the 2 ways a signal is terminated?","Enzyme degradation; Reuptake/uptake from cleft"]]},{"id":31013,"t":"Adrenergic Transmission","s":"What does “re”-uptake mean, as opposed to “uptake”?","o":["Taken back by the same cell that released it","Taken up by any neighbouring cell, neuronal or not, after release","Taken into the blood","Broken down by enzymes","Moved into the postsynaptic cell"],"e":"Reuptake: “re” because it is the same cell that released it; otherwise it is just “uptake” (e.g. by neighbouring non-neuronal cells, a minority for NA).","w":["That is uptake.","Not.","Degradation.","Not."],"k":"Reuptake = same cell.","rn":[["","Reuptake vs uptake?","Reuptake vs uptake?","Reuptake: releasing cell; Uptake: different cell"]]},{"id":31014,"t":"Adrenergic Transmission","s":"Which statement about catecholamine synthesis is correct?","o":["Dopamine → NA → adrenaline on one pathway","NA → dopamine → adrenaline, with each made by a separate pathway in different neurons","Adrenaline → NA → dopamine on one pathway","Each is made from choline","They share no enzymes"],"e":"Dopamine, noradrenaline and adrenaline share the same synthesis pathway (tyrosine → DOPA → dopamine → NA → Adr). Synthesis stops at a point that depends on the enzymes present in the cell. Enzymes and precursors are for Year 2.","w":["Wrong order; one pathway.","Reversed.","ACh uses choline.","Same pathway."],"k":"DA → NA → Adr.","rn":[["","Dopamine, noradrenaline and adrenaline share what?","Dopamine, noradrenaline and adrenaline share what?","The same synthesis pathway"],["","What is the order of the catecholamine pathway from tyrosine?","What is the order of the catecholamine pathway from tyrosine?","Tyrosine, DOPA, dopamine, noradrenaline, adrenaline"],["","What is the order of the catecholamine pathway from dopamine?","What is the order of the catecholamine pathway from dopamine?","Dopamine, then noradrenaline, then adrenaline"]]},{"id":31015,"t":"Adrenergic Transmission","s":"What determines whether a cell’s synthesis stops at dopamine, noradrenaline or adrenaline?","o":["The enzymes present in that cell","The receptors present on the postsynaptic cell receiving the transmitter","The amount of calcium entering","The rate of reuptake","The amount of MAO"],"e":"Synthesis stops at a point that depends on the enzymes present in the neuron/cell: some neurons go to adrenaline, some stop at noradrenaline, some at dopamine.","w":["Not.","Release.","Termination.","Termination."],"k":"Enzymes decide end product.","rn":[["","What decides where synthesis stops in a neuron?","What decides where synthesis stops in a neuron?","Enzymes present in the cell"]]},{"id":31016,"t":"The ANS and Its Transmitters","s":"Name the cells of the adrenal medulla that secrete adrenaline into the blood.","e":"Chromaffin cells (no postganglionic neuron) release mainly adrenaline (~80%).","k":"Chromaffin cells.","a":["chromaffin cells","chromaffin cell","chromaffin"],"rn":[["","What are the adrenal medulla cells called?","What are the adrenal medulla cells called?","Chromaffin cells"],["","What mainly secretes adrenaline?","What mainly secretes adrenaline?","Chromaffin cells; Adrenal medulla"],["","What do chromaffin cells release into the bloodstream?","What do chromaffin cells release into the bloodstream?","Adrenaline and noradrenaline; About 80% adrenaline"]]},{"id":31017,"t":"Adrenergic Transmission","s":"Name the presynaptic adrenoceptor subtype that inhibits further noradrenaline release.","e":"α2 (Gαi) lowers cAMP and Ca²⁺ entry, reducing NA release; blocking it increases release.","k":"α2.","a":["alpha2","α2","alpha 2","alpha-2","a2","alpha2 adrenoceptor"]},{"id":31018,"t":"Adrenergic Transmission","s":"Activation of which β-adrenoceptor subtype relaxes the bladder?","e":"β3: adipose tissue, bladder (relaxation), gallbladder.","k":"β3.","a":["beta3","β3","beta 3","beta-3","b3"]},{"id":31019,"t":"Adrenergic Transmission","s":"How does the order of agonist potency differ between α and β adrenoceptors?","o":["α: NA, Adr, isoprenaline; β: isoprenaline, Adr, NA","α: isoprenaline, Adr, NA; β: NA, Adr, isoprenaline, so NA is strongest at β","Both: NA, Adr, isoprenaline","Both: isoprenaline first","There is no order"],"e":"Agonists are listed from most to least potent. α: NA, Adr, isoprenaline. β: isoprenaline, Adr, NA. Isoprenaline is a synthetic derivative of noradrenaline used as a pharmacological tool.","w":["Swapped.","They differ.","Only β.","There is."],"k":"Agonist order α vs β.","rn":[["","What is isoprenaline?","What is isoprenaline?","Synthetic derivative of noradrenaline; Pharmacological tool"],["","What are the 3 α-adrenoceptor agonists (most to least potent)?","What are the 3 α-adrenoceptor agonists (most to least potent)?","Noradrenaline; Adrenaline; Isoprenaline"],["","What are the 3 β-adrenoceptor agonists (most to least potent)?","What are the 3 β-adrenoceptor agonists (most to least potent)?","Isoprenaline; Adrenaline; Noradrenaline"]]},{"id":31020,"t":"Adrenergic Transmission","s":"What is isoprenaline?","o":["A synthetic derivative of NA used as a tool","An endogenous catecholamine released by chromaffin cells with adrenaline","The main β antagonist","A muscarinic agonist","An enzyme that degrades NA"],"e":"Isoprenaline is a synthetic derivative of noradrenaline and a pharmacological tool – not endogenous, just a very good agonist (most potent at β).","w":["Not endogenous.","Agonist.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Isoprenaline: synthetic tool.","rn":[["","What is isoprenaline?","What is isoprenaline?","Synthetic derivative of noradrenaline; Pharmacological tool"]]},{"id":31021,"t":"Adrenergic Transmission","s":"Which pairing of adrenoceptor and G protein is correct?","o":["α1 – Gαq; α2 – Gαi; β – Gαs","α1 – Gαs; α2 – Gαq; β – Gαi, so β lowers cAMP","All α and β – Gαs","α1 – Gαi; α2 – Gαq; β – Gαs","α – ion channel; β – Gαq"],"e":"α1: Gαq (PLC +, IP3 and DAG). α2: Gαi (adenylyl cyclase −; also modulates Ca²⁺ and K⁺ channels – hence negative feedback). β1/β2/β3: Gαs (adenylyl cyclase +, cAMP). Memory aid: q-i-s.","w":["Wrong pairings.","Not.","α1/α2 swapped.","GPCRs."],"k":"α1 q, α2 i, β s.","rn":[["","α1 vs α2 vs β: G protein","α1 vs α2 vs β: G protein","α1: Gαq; α2: Gαi; β1/2/3: Gαs"]]},{"id":31022,"t":"Adrenergic Transmission","s":"How does presynaptic α2 activation reduce noradrenaline release?","o":["Gαi lowers cAMP and stops calcium entering","Gαs raises cAMP and increases calcium entry, which exhausts the vesicles","It opens nicotinic channels","It activates MAO","It blocks NET"],"e":"When NA binds presynaptic α2 it “tells the neuron to decrease its release”: α2 is Gαi, so it decreases cAMP and stops calcium entering, so release falls – negative feedback and autocrine signalling.","w":["α2 is Gαi.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"α2: ↓cAMP, ↓Ca²⁺ → ↓ release.","rn":[["","How does α2 decrease NA release?","How does α2 decrease NA release?","Gαi lowers cAMP; Stops calcium entry; Less vesicle release"]]},{"id":31023,"t":"Adrenergic Transmission","s":"Why is a non-selective α drug described as not useful?","o":["α1 and α2 actions contradict each other","α receptors are found only in the brain, so peripheral effects are not possible","α drugs have no effect on blood vessels","α receptors are not GPCRs","α receptors cannot be blocked"],"e":"Because α1 and α2 actions contradict each other, a non-selective α drug is not useful. This is shown again in the hypertension example (phentolamine).","w":["Blood vessels.","They do.","They are.","They can."],"k":"α1 vs α2 contradict.","rn":[["","Why is a non-selective α drug not useful?","Why is a non-selective α drug not useful?","α1 and α2 actions; Contradict each other"]]},{"id":31024,"t":"Adrenergic Transmission","s":"Why do selective drugs for adrenoceptor subtypes exist, given oral drugs go everywhere?","o":["The subtypes are structurally different","The subtypes are in different places, so location alone gives selectivity","Endogenous NA is selective for each subtype","All subtypes are identical","Only injected drugs are selective"],"e":"Endogenous NA and Adr are non-selective, but drugs selective for each subtype exist because the subtypes are structurally different (location alone could not give selectivity, as oral drugs go everywhere). At too high a dose a β2 drug starts binding β1.","w":["Location alone couldn’t.","Non-selective.","Different.","Not."],"k":"Subtype selectivity: structure."},{"id":31025,"t":"Adrenergic Transmission","s":"A musician takes propranolol before a concert. What effect is intended?","o":["Stopping tremor – peripheral signs of stress","Increasing heart rate and alertness by stimulating β1 in the heart","Bronchodilation","Stopping sweating via muscarinic block","Increasing glucose release"],"e":"Propranolol (β antagonist) is taken by musicians or speakers to stop the tremor and trembling voice of stress: it stops the peripheral signs of stress by antagonising the sympathetic system. (She advised against taking it.)","w":["It blocks β.","Blocking β2 can cause bronchoconstriction.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Propranolol: stops stress tremor.","rn":[["","Why can propranolol stop a performer's tremor?","Why can propranolol stop a performer's tremor?","Blocks β receptors; Antagonises sympathetic stress signs"]]},{"id":31026,"t":"Adrenergic Transmission","s":"A patient takes propranolol for a long time. What happens to β receptors, and why does it matter?","o":["They are upregulated, building tolerance","They are downregulated, so propranolol becomes more effective over time","They are destroyed permanently","They convert into α receptors","Nothing changes"],"e":"Block β receptors with propranolol for a long time and the body upregulates them (“I am tired of being blocked”), so NA has more places to act and tolerance builds. A long-term β agonist leads to downregulation – again tolerance.","w":["Upregulation with blockers.","Not.","Not.","It changes."],"k":"Long-term blocker → upregulation."},{"id":31027,"t":"Adrenergic Transmission","s":"Which pairing of receptor location and effect is correct?","o":["β1 – heart – increased rate and force","β1 – bronchi – dilation, as β1 dominates in airway smooth muscle","α1 – blood vessels – dilation","β3 – bladder – contraction","α2 – heart – increased rate"],"e":"α1 blood vessels: vasoconstriction. β1 heart: increased heart rate and force. β2 bronchi/vessels: dilation/relaxation. β2 skeletal muscle: tremor, glycogenolysis. β3 adipose: lipolysis, thermogenesis; bladder: relaxation. α2 presynaptic: decreased release.","w":["β2.","Constriction.","Relaxation.","Presynaptic."],"k":"β1: heart rate and force.","rn":[["","What does β1 do in the heart?","What does β1 do in the heart?","Increases heart rate; Increases force of contraction"]]},{"id":31028,"t":"Adrenergic Transmission","s":"Β2 receptors in skeletal muscle mediate which effects?","o":["Tremor and glycogenolysis","Increased force of contraction of the heart and raised heart rate","Lipolysis and thermogenesis","Vasoconstriction","Decreased NA release"],"e":"β2 in skeletal muscle: tremor and glycogenolysis. This explains tremor as a side effect of β2 agonists such as salbutamol.","w":["β1 heart.","β3.","α1.","α2."],"k":"β2 muscle: tremor.","rn":[["","What does β2 do in skeletal muscle?","What does β2 do in skeletal muscle?","Tremor; Glycogenolysis"]]},{"id":31029,"t":"Adrenergic Transmission","s":"Β3 activation in the bladder causes which effect?","o":["Relaxation (preventing urination)","Contraction of bladder smooth muscle, emptying the bladder","Increased heart rate","Bronchoconstriction","Increased salivation"],"e":"β3 in the bladder causes relaxation (preventing urination); in adipose tissue, lipolysis and thermogenesis. Now there are drugs targeting β3, e.g. for the bladder.","w":["Muscarinic.","β1.","Muscarinic.","Muscarinic."],"k":"β3 bladder: relaxation.","rn":[["","What does β3 do in the bladder?","What does β3 do in the bladder?","Relaxation; Prevents urination"]]},{"id":31030,"t":"Adrenergic Transmission","s":"Name the transporter that terminates noradrenaline signalling by reuptake into the presynaptic neuron.","e":"NET reuptake is the main route of NA termination in the periphery; cocaine and amphetamines block it.","k":"NET.","a":["net","noradrenaline transporter","norepinephrine transporter","uptake 1","uptake-1"],"rn":[["","What is the main mechanism that ends NA action?","What is the main mechanism that ends NA action?","Reuptake by NAT (NET)"],["","What else do antidepressants and cocaine inhibit besides NET (noradrenaline transporter)?","What else do antidepressants and cocaine inhibit besides NET (noradrenaline transporter)?","Other monoamine transporters"],["","What does NAT (NET) stand for?","What does NAT (NET) stand for?","Noradrenaline (norepinephrine) transporter"]]},{"id":31031,"t":"Adrenergic Transmission","s":"Why does cocaine cause a pounding heartbeat?","o":["It inhibits NET, so NA stays in the cleft","It blocks β1 receptors","It inhibits acetylcholinesterase","It releases NA from vesicles only","It activates muscarinic receptors"],"e":"Antidepressants and cocaine inhibit NET. With cocaine, NA stays in the cleft and the sympathetic system drives the heart; centrally it causes alertness, then a crash. Amphetamines inhibit the transporter and release non-vesicular NA, so their effect is even greater.","w":["Cocaine inhibits NET.","Not.","Amphetamines also release NA.","Not."],"k":"Cocaine: NET inhibition.","rn":[["","What 2 drug groups inhibit NET?","What 2 drug groups inhibit NET?","Antidepressants; Cocaine"],["","What else do antidepressants and cocaine inhibit besides NET (noradrenaline transporter)?","What else do antidepressants and cocaine inhibit besides NET (noradrenaline transporter)?","Other monoamine transporters"]]},{"id":31032,"t":"Drugs and the ANS","s":"How do amphetamines differ from cocaine?","o":["They block NET and release non-vesicular NA","They only inhibit MAO and COMT","They block adrenoceptors","They inhibit acetylcholinesterase","They have no effect on NA"],"e":"Amphetamines are “even smarter”: they inhibit the transporter and release NA that is not in vesicles, so their effect is even greater than cocaine’s.","w":["They block NET and release NA.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Amphetamines: block NET + release.","rn":[["","Why is the BP drop from a non-selective α antagonist not sustained?","Why is the BP drop from a non-selective α antagonist not sustained?","Blocks α2 feedback; More NA released; Competes at α1"]]},{"id":31033,"t":"Adrenergic Transmission","s":"Name one enzyme (other than COMT) that metabolises noradrenaline, adrenaline, dopamine and serotonin.","e":"MAO and COMT metabolise monoamines, but reuptake by NET is the main peripheral termination route.","k":"MAO.","a":["mao","monoamine oxidase"],"rn":[["","What 2 enzymes metabolise NA and Adr?","What 2 enzymes metabolise NA and Adr?","MAO; COMT"],["","Are NA, adrenaline, dopamine and serotonin ended by the same enzymes?","Are NA, adrenaline, dopamine and serotonin ended by the same enzymes?","Yes (MAO and COMT); Plus specific reuptake transporters"]]},{"id":31034,"t":"Adrenergic Transmission","s":"Antidepressants that inhibit NET are CNS drugs. Why do they matter for the ANS?","o":["Their adverse effects relate to peripheral ANS action","They have no peripheral effects at all because they act only in the brain","They are first-line asthma drugs","They block nicotinic receptors","They inhibit AChE"],"e":"Although these are CNS drugs, their adverse effects will relate to their peripheral action on the ANS (NET is also in the periphery).","w":["They do.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"NET inhibitors: peripheral ADRs.","rn":[["","Why do antidepressants cause ANS adverse effects?","Why do antidepressants cause ANS adverse effects?","Peripheral action on ANS"]]},{"id":31035,"t":"Cholinergic Transmission","s":"Name the enzyme that mainly terminates acetylcholine signalling in the synapse.","e":"Acetylcholinesterase in the synaptic cleft breaks down ACh.","k":"Acetylcholinesterase.","a":["acetylcholinesterase","ache","cholinesterase","cholinesterases"],"rn":[["","Where is ACh (acetylcholine) mainly broken down?","Where is ACh (acetylcholine) mainly broken down?","In the synaptic cleft; By acetylcholinesterase"]]},{"id":31036,"t":"Cholinergic Transmission","s":"Cholinesterase inhibitors are a main target for which condition?","o":["Alzheimer’s / dementia","Asthma, by increasing ACh in the airways to relax them","Hypertension, as first line","Parkinson’s","Depression"],"e":"Cholinesterases are the main target of drugs for Alzheimer’s/dementia: inhibiting them increases ACh, which seems to help memory, at least stopping progression (not reversing).","w":["ACh causes bronchoconstriction.","Not first line.","Dopamine.","Serotonin."],"k":"AChE inhibitors: dementia.","rn":[["","Cholinesterase inhibitors are a main target for which conditions?","Cholinesterase inhibitors are a main target for which conditions?","Alzheimer's and dementia"]]},{"id":31037,"t":"Cholinergic Transmission","s":"Which statement compares muscarinic and nicotinic receptors correctly?","o":["Muscarinic: GPCR, M1–M5; nicotinic: pentameric cation channel","Muscarinic: pentameric cation channel; nicotinic: GPCR with five subtypes N1–N5","Both are GPCRs","Both are ion channels","Muscarinic: kinase-linked"],"e":"Muscarinic (mAChR): metabotropic GPCR, M1–M5, postsynaptic in smooth muscle, cardiac muscle and glands; agonists ACh, muscarine; antagonist atropine. Nicotinic (nAChR): ionotropic pentameric cation channel in autonomic ganglia, motor endplate, CNS; agonists ACh, nicotine; antagonist curare.","w":["Swapped.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Muscarinic GPCR; nicotinic channel.","rn":[["","Muscarinic vs nicotinic: receptor type","Muscarinic vs nicotinic: receptor type","Muscarinic: metabotropic (GPCR); Nicotinic: ionotropic (pentameric cation channel)"]]},{"id":31038,"t":"Cholinergic Transmission","s":"Which receptor type is found at autonomic ganglia, the motor endplate and in the CNS?","e":"Nicotinic receptors are ligand-gated ion channels (Na⁺ entry, depolarisation); curare blocks them.","k":"Nicotinic.","a":["nicotinic","nicotinic receptor","nicotinic acetylcholine receptor","nachr","nicotinic receptors"],"rn":[["","Where are nicotinic receptors found?","Where are nicotinic receptors found?","Autonomic ganglia; Motor endplate; CNS"]]},{"id":31039,"t":"Cholinergic Transmission","s":"Name the muscarinic antagonist derived from Atropa belladonna, used as an antidote for muscarinic poisoning.","e":"Atropine blocks muscarinic receptors.","k":"Atropine.","a":["atropine"],"rn":[["","What is the muscarinic antagonist?","What is the muscarinic antagonist?","Atropine (belladonna)"],["","What is atropine still used for?","What is atropine still used for?","Antidote to poisoning; At muscarinic receptors"]]},{"id":31040,"t":"Cholinergic Transmission","s":"Why can curare kill at higher concentrations?","o":["It blocks nicotinic signalling at NMJ, ganglia and CNS","It overstimulates muscarinic receptors","It inhibits NET","It activates α1","It inhibits MAO"],"e":"Curare (tubocurarine) is a nicotinic antagonist, used on blow-dart arrows because it paralyses. At higher concentrations it stops all fast signalling at the motor end plate and autonomic ganglia (so all sympathetic and parasympathetic output) and the CNS.","w":["Curare is a nicotinic antagonist.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Curare: nicotinic block.","rn":[["","What does curare do?","What does curare do?","Paralyses; Blocks fast signalling"],["","Where are nicotinic receptors found?","Where are nicotinic receptors found?","Autonomic ganglia; Motor endplate; CNS"],["","Why are nicotinic receptors in the autonomic ganglia?","Why are nicotinic receptors in the autonomic ganglia?","Fast positive signalling; Signal must go through"]]},{"id":31041,"t":"Cholinergic Transmission","s":"Which pairing of muscarinic subtype and second messenger is correct?","o":["M2 – Gαi, AC (−), increased gK⁺","M2 – Gαq, PLC (+), IP3 and DAG, reduced gK⁺","M1 – Gαi, AC (−)","M3 – Gαs, AC (+)","M1 – ion channel"],"e":"M1 (‘neural’): Gαq, PLC+, IP3, DAG, reduced gK⁺. M2 (‘cardiac’): Gαi, AC−, increased gK⁺. M3 (glandular/smooth muscle): Gαq, PLC+, IP3, DAG, ↑Ca²⁺. Nicotinic: ion channel. M4 and M5: CNS.","w":["That is M1.","Gαq.","Gαq.","Nicotinic."],"k":"M2: Gαi, ↑gK⁺."},{"id":31042,"t":"Cholinergic Transmission","s":"Muscarinic activation in the periphery causes which set of effects?","o":["Bronchoconstriction, slower heart, gut contraction, salivation","Bronchodilation, faster heart, gut relaxation","Vasoconstriction and glycogenolysis","Lipolysis and thermogenesis","Tremor"],"e":"Muscarinic activation: airway smooth muscle – bronchoconstriction; heart – decreased rate; gut smooth muscle – contraction; salivary glands – increased salivation; bladder smooth muscle – contraction. These link mainly with the parasympathetic.","w":["Those are sympathetic effects.","α1 / liver.","β3.","β2."],"k":"Muscarinic: rest and digest.","rn":[["","What does muscarinic activation do in gut smooth muscle?","What does muscarinic activation do in gut smooth muscle?","Contraction"]]},{"id":31043,"t":"Neurotransmitter Basics","s":"Why is the muscarinic effect table much simpler than the adrenoceptor one?","o":["Cholinergic ANS drugs are less used","Muscarinic receptors have only one subtype in the periphery and one effect","Muscarinic receptors are not in the ANS","They have no effects","They are ion channels"],"e":"Cholinergic drugs in the ANS are less used than the others, and much is still unknown about the subtypes and their locations. For now you don’t need to know which subtype is in each location.","w":["M1–M5.","They are.","They do.","GPCRs."],"k":"Cholinergic drugs less used.","rn":[["","Do MAO/COMT blockers matter in the periphery?","Do MAO/COMT blockers matter in the periphery?","No drugs used there; Used for CNS conditions"],["","What does a cholinergic drug do?","What does a cholinergic drug do?","Increases acetylcholine transmission"],["","How should you predict a drug's ANS side effects?","How should you predict a drug's ANS side effects?","Think of ANS functions; Lion (sympathetic), resting dog (parasympathetic)"]]},{"id":31044,"t":"The ANS and Its Transmitters","s":"Which statement about the parasympathetic pathway is correct?","o":["Long preganglionic, short postganglionic; ACh → muscarinic at target","Short pre, long post; NA at target","Long preganglionic, short postganglionic; NA → adrenoceptors at target","One neuron only","Origin T1–L2"],"e":"Parasympathetic: preganglionic cell bodies in the brain stem or S2–4 (cranio-sacral), long, synapse at or near the organ, releasing ACh onto nicotinic receptors. Postganglionic: short, releasing ACh onto muscarinic receptors on smooth muscle, cardiac muscle and glands.","w":["That is the sympathetic pathway.","ACh.","Two neurons.","Sympathetic origin."],"k":"Parasympathetic: long pre, short post, ACh.","rn":[["","How long are parasympathetic preganglionic and postganglionic neurons?","How long are parasympathetic preganglionic and postganglionic neurons?","Preganglionic: long; Postganglionic: short"],["","How long are sympathetic preganglionic and postganglionic neurons?","How long are sympathetic preganglionic and postganglionic neurons?","Preganglionic: short; Postganglionic: long"]]},{"id":31045,"t":"The ANS and Its Transmitters","s":"Which statement about the sympathetic pathway is correct?","o":["Short preganglionic (ACh → nicotinic); long postganglionic (NA)","Long preganglionic (NA → adrenoceptors); short postganglionic (ACh → muscarinic)","Origin S2–4","Ganglia on the target organ","Postganglionic releases ACh in most organs"],"e":"Sympathetic: preganglionic cell bodies T1–L2 (thoracolumbar), short, synapse near the cord, releasing ACh onto nicotinic receptors. Postganglionic: cell bodies in the sympathetic chain, named ganglia or adrenal medulla; long; release NA onto adrenoceptors.","w":["Reversed.","Parasympathetic.","Near cord.","Except sweat glands."],"k":"Sympathetic: short pre, long post, NA.","rn":[["","How long are sympathetic preganglionic and postganglionic neurons?","How long are sympathetic preganglionic and postganglionic neurons?","Preganglionic: short; Postganglionic: long"],["","How long are parasympathetic preganglionic and postganglionic neurons?","How long are parasympathetic preganglionic and postganglionic neurons?","Preganglionic: long; Postganglionic: short"]]},{"id":31046,"t":"The ANS and Its Transmitters","s":"Why is the autonomic ganglion transmitter/receptor the same in both ANS divisions?","o":["Fast positive signalling needs nicotinic channels","Both use noradrenaline at the ganglion to ensure a slow, sustained signal","The ganglion uses muscarinic receptors in both","There are no ganglia","Ganglia use adrenoceptors"],"e":"At the ganglion you want quick, fast, positive signalling so the postganglionic neuron depolarises. “When you want fast positive signalling, you put an ion channel there”: ACh acts on nicotinic receptors, sodium enters, depolarisation, the signal continues.","w":["ACh.","Nicotinic.","There are.","Nicotinic."],"k":"Ganglion: ACh → nicotinic.","rn":[["","Why are nicotinic receptors in the autonomic ganglia?","Why are nicotinic receptors in the autonomic ganglia?","Fast positive signalling; Signal must go through"]]},{"id":31047,"t":"The ANS and Its Transmitters","s":"What is an autonomic ganglion?","o":["A structure of nerve cell bodies where pre- and postganglionic neurons synapse","A structure of axon terminals on an effector organ releasing transmitter","A receptor type","A gland releasing adrenaline","A section of spinal cord"],"e":"Ganglia = a structure containing several nerve cell bodies, where preganglionic neurons synapse with postganglionic neurons.","w":["Not.","Not.","Adrenal medulla.","Not."],"k":"Ganglion definition.","rn":[["","What is a ganglion?","What is a ganglion?","Several nerve cell bodies; Pre- meets postganglionic"]]},{"id":31048,"t":"The ANS and Its Transmitters","s":"Why is the adrenal medulla a special case?","o":["It has no postganglionic neuron; chromaffin cells release Adr into blood","It has a parasympathetic supply that releases ACh into the blood","Its preganglionic neuron is adrenergic","It releases mostly NA","It has muscarinic receptors only"],"e":"The ANS is always a two-neuron system except the adrenal medulla: a short cholinergic preganglionic neuron, but postganglionic axons never develop. Chromaffin cells take an endocrine role, releasing adrenaline and noradrenaline (about 80% adrenaline) into the bloodstream – fight or flight.","w":["Sympathetic.","Cholinergic.","80% Adr.","Not."],"k":"Adrenal medulla: no postganglionic.","rn":[["","Image card: Why is this a special case of sympathetic supply? Which cells release the hormone?","Why is this a special case of sympathetic supply? Which cells release the hormone?","No postganglionic neuron; Chromaffin cells; About 80% adrenaline (+ noradrenaline)"]]},{"id":31049,"t":"The ANS and Its Transmitters","s":"Why are sympathetic effects on sweat glands mediated by muscarinic receptors?","o":["Their postganglionic neurones are cholinergic","They have parasympathetic supply only","They have adrenoceptors that bind ACh","They are supplied by the adrenal medulla","They lack ganglia"],"e":"In this special case the postganglionic neurones are also cholinergic (ACh → ACh), and the sweat glands only have muscarinic receptors – even though the supply is sympathetic.","w":["The supply is sympathetic.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Sweat glands: cholinergic sympathetic.","rn":[["","Why is the sweat gland a special case?","Why is the sweat gland a special case?","Postganglionic is cholinergic; ACh on muscarinic receptors"]]},{"id":31050,"t":"The ANS and Its Transmitters","s":"A poisoned patient shows parasympathetic signs and is NOT sweating. What does this point to?","o":["A problem beyond parasympathetic transmission, e.g. muscarinic receptors","A purely parasympathetic problem","A problem with NET","Normal physiology","A β1 problem"],"e":"If a poisoned patient can still sweat, their sympathetic supply is still working. If they show parasympathetic problems and are not sweating, it cannot be just parasympathetic transmission, because sweating is sympathetic – it might be something at the muscarinic receptors.","w":["Sweating is sympathetic, so it is not purely parasympathetic.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"No sweating → muscarinic issue?","rn":[["","A poisoned patient with parasympathetic signs who cannot sweat: what does it suggest?","A poisoned patient with parasympathetic signs who cannot sweat: what does it suggest?","Not just parasympathetic transmission; e.g. muscarinic receptor problem"]]},{"id":31051,"t":"ANS Functions","s":"A person faces a lion. Which set of effects is sympathetic?","o":["Glucose release, bronchodilation, faster heart, sphincters contracted","Bronchoconstriction, slower heart, digestion","Increased salivation and gut contraction","Pupil accommodation and digestion","Decreased glucose release"],"e":"Fight or flight: glucose released, bronchodilation, heart rate increases, GI smooth muscle relaxed, bladder relaxed but sphincters contracted, eyes open. The dog (rest and digest) is the opposite.","w":["That is the dog (parasympathetic).","Parasympathetic.","Parasympathetic.","Not."],"k":"Lion = sympathetic.","rn":[["","In fight or flight, GI and bladder smooth muscle vs sphincters?","In fight or flight, GI and bladder smooth muscle vs sphincters?","Smooth muscle relaxed; Sphincters contracted"],["","Which adrenoceptors contract GI sphincters and the bladder sphincter?","Which adrenoceptors contract GI sphincters and the bladder sphincter?","α1 and β2"]]},{"id":31052,"t":"Adrenergic Transmission","s":"Which statement about sympathetic and parasympathetic effects on the heart is correct?","o":["Both change rate; only sympathetic increases force","Both change rate and force equally","Only parasympathetic changes force","Neither affects rate","Sympathetic slows rate"],"e":"Both change heart rate (sympathetic raises, parasympathetic slows), but only the sympathetic also increases inotropic force; the parasympathetic has no direct effect on force. The two are not always opponents.","w":["Only the sympathetic changes force.","Sympathetic.","Both do.","Raises."],"k":"Force: sympathetic only.","rn":[["","What does β1 do in the heart?","What does β1 do in the heart?","Increases heart rate; Increases force of contraction"]]},{"id":31053,"t":"Drugs and the ANS","s":"Which statement is TRUE?","o":["The synaptic uptake of NA can be inhibited selectively","The sympathetic division uses adrenaline as its primary transmitter at postganglionic endings","The two divisions have opposite effects in every organ","There are two subtypes of β-adrenoceptor","NA termination is mainly by MAO and COMT"],"e":"True: NET can be inhibited by antidepressants. False: NA is the main postganglionic transmitter; the divisions are not opposite in every organ (e.g. lens of the eye); there are three β subtypes; NA termination is mainly reuptake.","w":["NA is.","Not every organ.","Three.","Reuptake."],"k":"NET can be inhibited selectively.","rn":[["","Can noradrenaline uptake be inhibited selectively?","Can noradrenaline uptake be inhibited selectively?","Yes; e.g. by antidepressants"]]},{"id":31055,"t":"Adrenergic Transmission","s":"Sympathetic stimulation of the gut has what effect on motility and sphincters?","o":["Slows motility; contracts sphincters","Slows motility and relaxes the sphincters so contents pass slowly","Speeds motility; relaxes sphincters","Speeds motility; contracts sphincters","No effect"],"e":"Sympathetic: slows GI transit and constricts sphincters. Parasympathetic: speeds GI transit and relaxes sphincters. (T/F: “Sympathetic stimulation inhibits gut motility and sphincter tone” is false – it increases sphincter tone.)","w":["Sphincters contract.","Parasympathetic.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Sympathetic gut: slow, sphincters tight.","rn":[["","Which adrenoceptors contract GI sphincters and the bladder sphincter?","Which adrenoceptors contract GI sphincters and the bladder sphincter?","α1 and β2"],["","In fight or flight, GI and bladder smooth muscle vs sphincters?","In fight or flight, GI and bladder smooth muscle vs sphincters?","Smooth muscle relaxed; Sphincters contracted"]]},{"id":31056,"t":"Drugs and the ANS","s":"An adrenoceptor agonist is described as which type of drug?","o":["Directly-acting sympathomimetic","Indirectly-acting sympathomimetic, as it changes release of NA","Sympatholytic","Parasympathomimetic","Parasympatholytic"],"e":"Sympathomimetic drugs stimulate: adrenoceptor agonists are directly-acting; altering release, metabolism or reuptake is indirectly-acting. Sympatholytic drugs inhibit (direct and indirect).","w":["Agonists are direct.","Inhibits.","ACh pathway.","ACh pathway."],"k":"Agonist = direct sympathomimetic.","rn":[["","Directly vs indirectly acting sympathomimetic","Directly vs indirectly acting sympathomimetic","Direct: adrenoceptor agonists; Indirect: alter release, metabolism, reuptake"]]},{"id":31057,"t":"Drugs and the ANS","s":"A drug that blocks NET to increase NA action is classed as:","o":["An indirectly-acting sympathomimetic","A directly-acting sympathomimetic, as it acts on adrenoceptors","A sympatholytic","A parasympathomimetic","A parasympatholytic"],"e":"Altering release, metabolism or reuptake to increase NA/Adr action is indirectly-acting sympathomimetic. Directly-acting means an adrenoceptor agonist.","w":["Not on receptors.","It increases.","Not.","Not."],"k":"NET block = indirect sympathomimetic.","rn":[["","Directly vs indirectly acting sympathomimetic","Directly vs indirectly acting sympathomimetic","Direct: adrenoceptor agonists; Indirect: alter release, metabolism, reuptake"]]},{"id":31058,"t":"Drugs and the ANS","s":"Neostigmine, an acetylcholinesterase inhibitor, is classed as what type of drug?","e":"Acetylcholinesterase inhibitors are (indirectly-acting) parasympathomimetics.","k":"Parasympathomimetic.","a":["parasympathomimetic","cholinomimetic","indirectly-acting parasympathomimetic","indirect cholinomimetic","cholinergic"],"rn":[["","What does parasympathomimetic mean?","What does parasympathomimetic mean?","Stimulates the parasympathetic; Also cholinomimetic / cholinergic"]]},{"id":31061,"t":"Drugs and the ANS","s":"A muscarinic agonist is considered to lower blood pressure by slowing the heart. Which adverse effects would be predicted?","o":["Bronchoconstriction, lacrimation, salivation, visual disturbance","Bronchodilation, dry mouth, urinary retention and tachycardia from blocked ACh","Tremor and glycogenolysis","Reflex tachycardia and headache only","Lipolysis and thermogenesis"],"e":"Stimulating the parasympathetic (muscarinic agonist, e.g. carbachol) decreases heart rate, but ADRs come from parasympathetic stimulation elsewhere: bronchoconstriction, lacrimation, salivation, visual disturbances, possibly incontinence. “Doesn’t sound good! Alternative system?”","w":["Those are anticholinergic effects.","β2 effects.","α antagonist ADRs.","β3."],"k":"Muscarinic agonist ADRs.","rn":[["","Parasympathetic stimulation for hypertension: what are the 4 ADRs?","Parasympathetic stimulation for hypertension: what are the 4 ADRs?","Bronchoconstriction; Lacrimation, salivation; Visual disturbances; Incontinence"]]},{"id":31062,"t":"Drugs and the ANS","s":"Why would a muscarinic agonist be completely contraindicated in an asthmatic patient?","o":["Bronchoconstriction could trigger an asthma attack","It would cause bronchodilation, masking an asthma attack until it is severe","It would block β2 receptors","It would raise heart rate","It causes cold extremities"],"e":"Parasympathetic stimulation causes bronchoconstriction, which in an asthmatic patient could trigger an asthma attack and eventually asphyxiation – completely contraindicated.","w":["Opposite.","Not.","Lowers.","β-blocker ADR."],"k":"Muscarinic agonist: asthma contraindicated.","rn":[["","Why is a muscarinic agonist contraindicated in asthma?","Why is a muscarinic agonist contraindicated in asthma?","Bronchoconstriction; Asthma attack"]]},{"id":31063,"t":"Drugs and the ANS","s":"To boost parasympathetic action on the heart, which receptor would you target, and why?","o":["Muscarinic – it is mainly the receptor in the heart","Nicotinic – acts fastest","β1 – it is the parasympathetic receptor","α2 – it is in the heart","β3 – it slows the heart"],"e":"Class reasoning: the parasympathetic decreases heart rate, so use an agonist. Muscarinic is the receptor mainly in the heart; a nicotinic drug would affect a lot more (ganglia, NMJ, CNS).","w":["A nicotinic drug would affect much more than the heart.","Sympathetic.","Presynaptic.","Not."],"k":"Heart parasympathetic: muscarinic.","rn":[["","Hypertension: muscarinic or nicotinic agonist?","Hypertension: muscarinic or nicotinic agonist?","Muscarinic; Receptor in the heart"],["","What does muscarinic activation do in the heart?","What does muscarinic activation do in the heart?","Decreased heart rate"]]},{"id":31064,"t":"Drugs and the ANS","s":"Phentolamine (a non-selective α antagonist) lowers blood pressure only briefly. Why?","o":["Blocking α2 removes feedback, so more NA competes at α1","α1 block increases NA reuptake","It blocks β1 as well","It is an agonist","It is metabolised instantly by MAO"],"e":"Blocking α1 on vessels reduces constriction, lowering BP. But it also blocks presynaptic α2, removing the negative feedback (“the police effect”), so more NA is released, which competes with the antagonist at α1 – like surmounting a competitive antagonist. So the effect is not sustained.","w":["Blocking α2 removes negative feedback.","Not.","Antagonist.","Not."],"k":"α2 block → more NA → competes at α1.","rn":[["","Why is the BP drop from a non-selective α antagonist not sustained?","Why is the BP drop from a non-selective α antagonist not sustained?","Blocks α2 feedback; More NA released; Competes at α1"]]},{"id":31065,"t":"Drugs and the ANS","s":"Which ADRs were listed for an α antagonist such as phentolamine?","o":["Postural hypotension, headache, reflex tachycardia","Bronchoconstriction, bradycardia, cold extremities and muscle fatigue","Dry mouth and urinary retention","Tremor","Bronchodilation"],"e":"α antagonist ADRs: postural hypotension, headache (a lot of α2 in the brain) and reflex tachycardia – “really not a great therapy”.","w":["Propranolol ADRs.","Anticholinergic.","β2 agonist.","Not."],"k":"α antagonist ADRs.","rn":[["","What are the 3 ADRs of α antagonists?","What are the 3 ADRs of α antagonists?","Postural hypotension; Headache; Reflex tachycardia"]]},{"id":31066,"t":"Drugs and the ANS","s":"Which ADRs were listed for propranolol?","o":["Bronchoconstriction, bradycardia, fatigue, cold extremities","Reflex tachycardia and headache","Tremor and tachycardia","Dry mouth and constipation","Increased salivation"],"e":"Non-selective β antagonist (propranolol) ADRs: bronchoconstriction, postural hypotension, bradycardia, muscle weakness/fatigue, cold extremities (because of β2 block). Non-selective β antagonists are not used for hypertension.","w":["Those are α antagonist ADRs.","β2 agonist.","Anticholinergic.","Muscarinic agonist."],"k":"Propranolol ADRs.","rn":[["","Non-selective β antagonist: 2 other ADRs?","Non-selective β antagonist: 2 other ADRs?","Muscle weakness/fatigue; Cold extremities"]]},{"id":31067,"t":"Drugs and the ANS","s":"Why do cold extremities occur with propranolol?","o":["It blocks β2 receptors on vessels","It blocks α1 receptors on vessels, causing vasodilation and heat loss","It activates muscarinic receptors","It inhibits NET","It blocks β1 in the heart only"],"e":"Propranolol blocks β1 and β2; β2 causes vasodilation, so blocking it leads to cold extremities.","w":["β2.","Not.","Not.","Non-selective."],"k":"Cold extremities: β2 block.","rn":[["","What do β2 receptors do in vessels?","What do β2 receptors do in vessels?","Vasodilation"]]},{"id":31068,"t":"Drugs and the ANS","s":"Which drug choice best lowers blood pressure with fewer side effects?","o":["A β1-selective antagonist (e.g. atenolol, bisoprolol)","A non-selective β antagonist such as propranolol, which blocks both β1 and β2","A non-selective α antagonist (phentolamine)","A muscarinic agonist (carbachol)","An acetylcholinesterase inhibitor"],"e":"β1-selective antagonists (atenolol, bisoprolol) decrease blood pressure with fewer side effects, e.g. bronchoconstriction – depending on dose.","w":["Blocks β2 too.","Not sustained.","Bronchoconstriction etc.","Same ADRs."],"k":"β1-selective antagonist.","rn":[["","What are 2 β1 antagonists?","What are 2 β1 antagonists?","Atenolol; Bisoprolol"]]},{"id":31069,"t":"Drugs and the ANS","s":"Why are even β1-selective antagonists not recommended in asthmatic patients?","o":["At higher levels they may also bind β2","They are β2 agonists that worsen asthma by overstimulation","They block muscarinic receptors","They increase NA release","They cause dry mouth"],"e":"At higher concentrations (too much drug, altered metabolism) β1 antagonists may bind β2 (“you eat a full plate and, still hungry, go for the food you like less”), so even β1 antagonists are not recommended in asthma.","w":["Antagonists.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Selectivity lost → β2 block."},{"id":31070,"t":"Drugs and the ANS","s":"What advantage would a drug with mixed β1 antagonist and β2 agonist activity have?","o":["It avoids β2 block, so it can suit asthmatic patients","It blocks both β1 and β2","It is first line for all hypertension","It has no effect on the heart","It stimulates muscarinic receptors"],"e":"A mixed β1 antagonist + β2 agonist overcomes the chance of the β1 drug acting at β2, making prescribing possible particularly in asthmatic patients. A couple exist, but they are not first line.","w":["It agonises β2.","Not first line.","β1 block.","Not."],"k":"Mixed β1 ant/β2 ag.","rn":[["","Advantage of mixed β1 antagonist / β2 agonist?","Advantage of mixed β1 antagonist / β2 agonist?","Avoids bronchoconstriction; Possible in asthmatics"]]},{"id":31071,"t":"Drugs and the ANS","s":"Which pairing of ANS effect on the bronchi is correct?","o":["Parasympathetic: constriction; sympathetic: dilation","Parasympathetic: dilation via muscarinic; sympathetic: constriction via α1","Both: constriction","Both: dilation","Neither affects bronchi"],"e":"Parasympathetic in the bronchi: bronchoconstriction. Sympathetic: bronchodilation.","w":["Reversed.","Not.","Not.","Both do."],"k":"Bronchi: para constricts, symp dilates.","rn":[["","Effect of each ANS division in the bronchi?","Effect of each ANS division in the bronchi?","Parasympathetic: bronchoconstriction; Sympathetic: bronchodilation"]]},{"id":31072,"t":"Drugs and the ANS","s":"Name the inhaled muscarinic antagonist that was one of the first ANS-acting asthma treatments.","e":"Ipratropium is an inhaled muscarinic antagonist; inhalation limits anticholinergic effects such as dry mouth and urinary retention.","k":"Ipratropium.","a":["ipratropium","ipratropium bromide"],"rn":[["","Which antimuscarinic was an early asthma treatment?","Which antimuscarinic was an early asthma treatment?","Ipratropium"]]},{"id":31073,"t":"Drugs and the ANS","s":"A patient on a muscarinic antagonist complains of dry mouth and cannot pass urine. What are these?","o":["Anticholinergic effects","Cholinergic effects from overstimulation of muscarinic receptors in the bladder","β2 agonist effects","α antagonist effects","Toxic effects only at overdose"],"e":"Muscarinic antagonist ADRs are well known as anticholinergic effects: blurred vision, dry mouth, urinary retention, constipation, tachycardia. Every time a drug has them, expect a high impact on quality of life.","w":["Opposite.","Tremor, tachycardia.","Headache, hypotension.","At normal doses."],"k":"Dry mouth, retention = anticholinergic.","rn":[["","Anticholinergic: 2 head ADRs?","Anticholinergic: 2 head ADRs?","Blurred vision; Dry mouth"],["","Anticholinergic: 3 body ADRs?","Anticholinergic: 3 body ADRs?","Urinary retention; Constipation; Tachycardia"]]},{"id":31074,"t":"Drugs and the ANS","s":"Why do inhaled muscarinic antagonists have fewer ADRs than predicted?","o":["Inhalation goes straight to the lungs, with less in the blood","They become agonists in the lungs","They act only on nicotinic receptors","They are not absorbed at all","They block NET"],"e":"Most asthma treatments are inhaled, going straight to the lungs with less in the bloodstream, so muscarinic antagonists have fewer adverse effects than you might predict.","w":["Inhalation keeps blood levels low.","Muscarinic.","Some is absorbed.","Not."],"k":"Inhaled: less systemic."},{"id":31075,"t":"Drugs and the ANS","s":"Name the first-line ANS-acting reliever for asthma, a β2 agonist.","e":"Salbutamol (β2 agonist) relaxes bronchial smooth muscle; at high doses it causes tremor (skeletal β2) and tachycardia (β1).","k":"Salbutamol.","a":["salbutamol","albuterol"],"rn":[["","What is the first-line β2 agonist?","What is the first-line β2 agonist?","Salbutamol"]]},{"id":31076,"t":"Adrenergic Transmission","s":"A patient using salbutamol develops tremor. Why?","o":["β2 receptors in skeletal muscle","β1 receptors in skeletal muscle, which are stimulated at all doses","Muscarinic receptors in muscle","α1 receptors in muscle","NET inhibition"],"e":"β2 in skeletal muscle mediates tremor and glycogenolysis; tremor and increased skeletal-muscle blood flow are due to β2 receptors there.","w":["β2.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Tremor: β2 in muscle.","rn":[["","What does β2 do in skeletal muscle?","What does β2 do in skeletal muscle?","Tremor; Glycogenolysis"]]},{"id":31077,"t":"Drugs and the ANS","s":"During an asthma crisis a patient takes many puffs with poor technique and swallows the drug. Why might tachycardia result?","o":["Too much β2 agonist enters the blood","The inhaler becomes a β2 antagonist when swallowed and blocks the heart","Salbutamol is a muscarinic agonist","It inhibits AChE","It blocks α2"],"e":"Tachycardia only happens with too much β2 agonist in the body – rare with an inhaler, but in an asthma crisis patients take puff after puff without proper technique, swallow the drug, it enters the bloodstream and can affect potassium levels and eventually heart rate.","w":["Not.","Agonist.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Swallowed β2 agonist → tachycardia.","rn":[["","Too much β2 agonist in the blood affects which 2 things?","Too much β2 agonist in the blood affects which 2 things?","Potassium levels; Heart rate"]]},{"id":31078,"t":"Drugs and the ANS","s":"What is the solution for using β2 agonists with very limited adverse effects?","o":["Inhalation","Oral tablets, which avoid the lungs and so avoid side effects","IV injection","Combining with propranolol","Using isoprenaline"],"e":"Solution for β2 agonists with (very) limited adverse effects: inhalation.","w":["Systemic.","Systemic.","Opposes.","Tool."],"k":"Inhaled β2.","rn":[["","How are β2 agonist ADRs kept very limited?","How are β2 agonist ADRs kept very limited?","Inhalation"]]},{"id":31079,"t":"Drugs and the ANS","s":"A β1-acting drug is prescribed. Which non-cardiac effect might occur at high concentration?","o":["Interaction with glucose levels","Increased salivation and gut motility from parasympathetic action","Bronchoconstriction from muscarinic activation","Decreased tremor","Incontinence"],"e":"A β1 drug will increase heart rate but, at high concentration, may act on other receptors and may interact with glucose levels – important for diabetic patients.","w":["Parasympathetic.","Muscarinic.","Not.","Not."],"k":"β1 high dose: glucose effects."},{"id":31080,"t":"Adrenergic Transmission","s":"A drug blocks presynaptic α2 receptors only. What would you predict for NA release?","o":["It would increase","It would decrease, because α2 normally stimulates NA release","It would stop","No change","It would switch to ACh"],"e":"Presynaptic α2 provides negative feedback on NA release (Gαi, ↓cAMP, ↓Ca²⁺). Blocking it removes this, so more NA is released (the problem with non-selective α antagonists).","w":["α2 inhibits release.","Not.","It changes.","Not."],"k":"α2 block → ↑ NA release."},{"id":31081,"t":"Drugs and the ANS","s":"A patient takes a drug that inhibits acetylcholinesterase. Which effects would you predict in the periphery?","o":["More parasympathetic effects: slow heart, salivation, constriction","More sympathetic effects: fast heart, dry mouth","No peripheral effects","Only CNS effects","Tremor"],"e":"AChE inhibitors stop ACh metabolism, keeping it longer in the cleft and increasing its action – like stimulating the parasympathetic: bradycardia, salivation, lacrimation, bronchoconstriction, visual disturbance.","w":["Increasing ACh gives parasympathetic effects.","Peripheral too.","Not.","β2."],"k":"AChE inhibitor → parasympathetic.","rn":[["","Parasympathetic stimulation for hypertension: what are the 4 ADRs?","Parasympathetic stimulation for hypertension: what are the 4 ADRs?","Bronchoconstriction; Lacrimation, salivation; Visual disturbances; Incontinence"],["","How should you predict a drug's ANS side effects?","How should you predict a drug's ANS side effects?","Think of ANS functions; Lion (sympathetic), resting dog (parasympathetic)"],["","Effect of each ANS division in the bronchi?","Effect of each ANS division in the bronchi?","Parasympathetic: bronchoconstriction; Sympathetic: bronchodilation"]]},{"id":31082,"t":"Cholinergic Transmission","s":"Atropine is used as an antidote. For intoxication with which type of drug?","o":["Drugs that act a lot on muscarinic receptors","Drugs that block muscarinic receptors, such as other anticholinergics","β2 agonists","NET inhibitors","α antagonists"],"e":"Atropine is still used, e.g. as an antidote for intoxication with drugs that act a lot on muscarinic receptors, but must be used carefully because it acts on all muscarinic receptors.","w":["It is one.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Atropine antidote.","rn":[["","What is atropine still used for?","What is atropine still used for?","Antidote to poisoning; At muscarinic receptors"],["","Why must atropine be used carefully?","Why must atropine be used carefully?","Acts on all muscarinic receptors; Very potent"]]},{"id":31083,"t":"Cholinergic Transmission","s":"A curare-like drug is given. Which systems would be affected?","o":["NMJ, both ANS divisions (ganglia) and CNS","Only the parasympathetic effector organs, through muscarinic block","Only β1 in the heart","Only sweat glands","Only the adrenal cortex"],"e":"Curare blocks nicotinic receptors at the motor end plate, the autonomic ganglia (so all sympathetic and parasympathetic output) and the CNS.","w":["Nicotinic.","Not.","Muscarinic.","Not."],"k":"Nicotinic block: widespread.","rn":[["","Where are nicotinic receptors found?","Where are nicotinic receptors found?","Autonomic ganglia; Motor endplate; CNS"],["","What are the 2 divisions of the ANS?","What are the 2 divisions of the ANS?","Sympathetic; Parasympathetic"],["","Which part of the pathway is the same in both divisions?","Which part of the pathway is the same in both divisions?","Autonomic ganglia; ACh on nicotinic"]]},{"id":31084,"t":"Neurotransmitter Basics","s":"Nicotinic receptor activation leads to which event?","o":["Na⁺ entry and depolarisation","Gαq activation, IP3 production and calcium release from stores","cAMP rise","K⁺ conductance increase via Gαi","Gene transcription"],"e":"Nicotinic = ionotropic, a ligand-gated ion channel letting sodium in (and potassium through) → depolarisation, so it excites a cell. When you want fast excitation, you put a nicotinic receptor there.","w":["Muscarinic M1/M3.","β.","M2.","Nuclear."],"k":"Nicotinic: Na⁺ in.","rn":[["","What triggers vesicle fusion and release?","What triggers vesicle fusion and release?","Depolarisation; Calcium entry"]]},{"id":31085,"t":"Adrenergic Transmission","s":"M2 receptor activation in the heart slows it. Which mechanism fits?","o":["Gαi: less cAMP and more K⁺ conductance","Gαq: more IP3 and calcium","Ion channel letting Na⁺ in","Gαs: more cAMP","NET block"],"e":"M2 (‘cardiac’): Gαi, adenylyl cyclase inhibited, increased gK⁺ (potassium conductance) – slowing the heart.","w":["M2 is Gαi.","Nicotinic.","β.","Not."],"k":"M2: Gαi, ↑gK⁺.","rn":[["","How does α2 decrease NA release?","How does α2 decrease NA release?","Gαi lowers cAMP; Stops calcium entry; Less vesicle release"]]},{"id":31086,"t":"Adrenergic Transmission","s":"A drug activates α1 receptors on blood vessels. What happens, and which G protein mediates it?","o":["Vasoconstriction via Gαq","Vasodilation via Gαs, lowering resistance","Vasoconstriction via Gαi","Vasodilation via Gαq","No effect"],"e":"α1 on blood vessels: smooth muscle contraction – vasoconstriction. α1 is Gαq (PLC+, IP3, DAG). The final effect depends on the tissue.","w":["β2.","α2.","Constriction.","It acts."],"k":"α1: Gq, vasoconstriction."},{"id":31087,"t":"Adrenergic Transmission","s":"A drug selectively activates β3. Which clinical target fits?","o":["The bladder","The heart, to increase heart rate in bradycardia","The bronchi","The salivary glands","The pupils"],"e":"When she was a student β3 had no drugs; now its locations are known and there are drugs, e.g. for the bladder (β3 relaxes the bladder, preventing urination).","w":["β1.","β2.","Muscarinic.","Not."],"k":"β3 drugs: bladder.","rn":[["","What does β3 do in the bladder?","What does β3 do in the bladder?","Relaxation; Prevents urination"]]},{"id":31088,"t":"The ANS and Its Transmitters","s":"Sympathetic stimulation causes sweating. Which receptor type mediates this?","e":"Sympathetic postganglionic neurones to sweat glands are cholinergic, acting on muscarinic receptors.","k":"Muscarinic.","a":["muscarinic","muscarinic receptor","muscarinic receptors","m3"],"rn":[["","Why is the sweat gland a special case?","Why is the sweat gland a special case?","Postganglionic is cholinergic; ACh on muscarinic receptors"]]},{"id":31089,"t":"The ANS and Its Transmitters","s":"What makes up about 80% of the adrenal medulla’s secretion?","o":["Adrenaline","Noradrenaline, with a small amount of adrenaline","Acetylcholine","Dopamine","Cortisol"],"e":"Chromaffin cells release adrenaline and noradrenaline into the bloodstream – about 80% adrenaline.","w":["Minority.","Preganglionic.","Not.","Cortex."],"k":"Adrenal medulla: ~80% Adr.","rn":[["","What do chromaffin cells release into the bloodstream?","What do chromaffin cells release into the bloodstream?","Adrenaline and noradrenaline; About 80% adrenaline"],["","What does the sympathetic make the adrenal medulla secrete?","What does the sympathetic make the adrenal medulla secrete?","Adrenaline and noradrenaline"]]},{"id":31090,"t":"The ANS and Its Transmitters","s":"Where do parasympathetic preganglionic cell bodies lie?","o":["Brain stem or S2–4 (cranio-sacral)","T1–L2 lateral horn (thoracolumbar), near the sympathetic chain","Sympathetic chain","Adrenal medulla","Near the target organ"],"e":"Parasympathetic preganglionic neurons: cell bodies in the grey matter of the brain stem or the lateral horn of S2–4 (cranio-sacral). Sympathetic: T1–L2 (thoracolumbar).","w":["Sympathetic.","Postganglionic sympathetic.","Not.","Postganglionic para."],"k":"Para origin: cranio-sacral.","rn":[["","Why is the parasympathetic called \"cranio-sacral\"?","Why is the parasympathetic called \"cranio-sacral\"?","Brain stem and S2-4 origin"]]},{"id":31091,"t":"The ANS and Its Transmitters","s":"Where do parasympathetic postganglionic cell bodies lie?","o":["In ganglia near or on the target organ","In the sympathetic chain next to the spinal cord, like sympathetic neurons","In the brain stem","In the adrenal cortex","In the lateral horn"],"e":"Parasympathetic postganglionic neurons have cell bodies in indistinct ganglia near or on their final target organ; they are short.","w":["Sympathetic.","Preganglionic.","Not.","Preganglionic."],"k":"Para ganglia: near organ.","rn":[["","Where are parasympathetic postganglionic cell bodies?","Where are parasympathetic postganglionic cell bodies?","Indistinct ganglia; Near or on target organ"]]},{"id":31092,"t":"Adrenergic Transmission","s":"A drug blocks NET peripherally. What effect on heart rate would you predict?","o":["Increase, as NA stays in the cleft","Decrease, as less NA reaches the heart receptors","No change","Decrease via muscarinic block","Increase via ACh"],"e":"NET inhibition (antidepressants, cocaine) keeps NA in the cleft, so the sympathetic drives the heart (pounding heartbeat with cocaine).","w":["More NA.","It changes.","Not.","Not."],"k":"NET block → ↑ HR."},{"id":31093,"t":"Cholinergic Transmission","s":"Which of the following is NOT a sympathetic effect?","o":["Increased salivation","Increased heart rate","Bronchodilation","Glucose release","Sphincter contraction"],"e":"Increased salivation is a muscarinic (parasympathetic) effect. Sympathetic: increased heart rate, bronchodilation, glucose release, sphincter contraction.","w":["Sympathetic.","Sympathetic.","Sympathetic.","Sympathetic."],"k":"Salivation: parasympathetic.","rn":[["","What does muscarinic activation do in salivary glands?","What does muscarinic activation do in salivary glands?","Increased salivation"]]},{"id":31094,"t":"Adrenergic Transmission","s":"Which receptors mediate liver glycogenolysis?","o":["α1 / β2","β1 only, as it is also in the heart and drives glucose release","Muscarinic","Nicotinic","α2"],"e":"In the liver (hepatocytes), α1/β2 mediate glycogenolysis.","w":["Not.","Not.","Not.","Presynaptic."],"k":"Liver glycogenolysis: α1/β2.","rn":[["","Which adrenoceptors cause glycogenolysis in the liver?","Which adrenoceptors cause glycogenolysis in the liver?","α1 and β2"]]},{"id":31095,"t":"ANS Functions","s":"In GI smooth muscle, α1/β2 activation causes what, and in sphincters?","o":["Smooth muscle relaxation; sphincter contraction","Smooth muscle contraction; sphincter relaxation, speeding transit","Both relax","Both contract","No effect"],"e":"α1/β2 in GI smooth muscle: relaxation. In GI and bladder sphincteric muscle: contraction.","w":["Reversed.","Sphincter contracts.","Smooth muscle relaxes.","It acts."],"k":"GI: relax; sphincters: contract.","rn":[["","In fight or flight, GI and bladder smooth muscle vs sphincters?","In fight or flight, GI and bladder smooth muscle vs sphincters?","Smooth muscle relaxed; Sphincters contracted"]]},{"id":31096,"t":"Adrenergic Transmission","s":"Why is it wrong to assume “β2 always means dilation”?","o":["The final effect depends on the tissue/cell","β2 receptors switch G protein between tissues, from Gs to Gq","β2 has no effects","β2 is an ion channel","β2 is only in the CNS"],"e":"The final effect (dilation, contraction, ion alterations) will depend on the tissue/cells. You cannot memorise that β2 always means dilation; link receptor, location and effect.","w":["Same G protein.","It does.","GPCR.","Periphery."],"k":"Effect depends on tissue.","rn":[["","What does the final effect of an adrenoceptor depend on?","What does the final effect of an adrenoceptor depend on?","The tissue / cells"]]},{"id":31097,"t":"Adrenergic Transmission","s":"Which statement about adrenergic transmission is correct?","o":["Every step is a drug target; in the ANS mainly receptors","Only reuptake is a drug target","Only synthesis is a target","No step is a target","Only MAO is a target"],"e":"Every step is a drug target! In the ANS, the drugs are mainly receptor agonists/antagonists.","w":["Every step is a target.","Not.","Every step.","CNS only."],"k":"Every step targetable.","rn":[["","Which steps of adrenergic transmission are drug targets?","Which steps of adrenergic transmission are drug targets?","Every step"]]},{"id":31098,"t":"Cholinergic Transmission","s":"Which statement is FALSE?","o":["ACh is metabolised by cholinesterases in the cytoplasm","NET can be inhibited selectively by antidepressants","Sympathetic gut stimulation increases sphincter tone","There is a third β subtype, β3","NA termination is mainly by reuptake"],"e":"False: within the synaptic cleft (not cytoplasm), ACh is mainly broken down by acetylcholinesterase. The others are true.","w":["True.","True.","True.","True."],"k":"ACh broken down in the cleft.","rn":[["","How is ACh signal mainly terminated?","How is ACh signal mainly terminated?","Metabolism by cholinesterases"]]},{"id":31099,"t":"Adrenergic Transmission","s":"“Both α1- and α2-adrenoceptor antagonists can be used to lower blood pressure.” Why is this false?","o":["Blocking presynaptic α2 increases NA release","α1 antagonists raise blood pressure by causing vasoconstriction","α2 is not presynaptic","α receptors are not on vessels","Both are agonists"],"e":"α1 antagonism on resistance vessels causes relaxation and lowers BP, but presynaptic α2 reduces NA release; blocking these autoreceptors increases NA release and tends to raise vascular resistance.","w":["They lower BP.","It is.","They are.","Not."],"k":"α2 block raises NA.","rn":[["","What does presynaptic α2 control?","What does presynaptic α2 control?","NA release"],["","Which adrenoceptor decreases neurotransmitter release from sympathetic neurones?","Which adrenoceptor decreases neurotransmitter release from sympathetic neurones?","Presynaptic α2"]]},{"id":31100,"t":"ANS Functions","s":"Why is the statement “the PNS and SNS have opposite effects in every organ” false?","o":["Some organs are controlled by only one system","Both systems always act in the same direction in every organ","Neither system reaches organs","Only the PNS has effects","They use the same transmitter"],"e":"The two have broadly opposing actions on many organs, but others are controlled by only one system (e.g. the lens of the eye). Kinetics also vary: PNS fine-tunes at rest; SNS produces rapid coordinated responses in an emergency.","w":["Not.","They do.","Both.","Different."],"k":"Not opposite everywhere."},{"id":31101,"t":"Adrenergic Transmission","s":"Name a non-selective β-blocker that is a directly-acting sympatholytic.","e":"Propranolol blocks β1 and β2; ADRs include bronchoconstriction, bradycardia, fatigue and cold extremities.","k":"Propranolol.","a":["propranolol"],"rn":[["","What is a β-adrenoceptor antagonist?","What is a β-adrenoceptor antagonist?","Propranolol"],["","Why is β1 selective better than propranolol?","Why is β1 selective better than propranolol?","Fewer side effects; e.g. less bronchoconstriction"]]},{"id":31102,"t":"Drugs and the ANS","s":"A drug inhibits NA release from sympathetic terminals. How is it classed?","o":["Indirectly-acting sympatholytic","Directly-acting sympathomimetic, as it acts on the terminal","Parasympathomimetic","Indirectly-acting sympathomimetic","Anticholinergic"],"e":"Indirect sympatholytic: decreasing release, increasing reuptake, anything that decreases sympathetic transmission without blocking receptors.","w":["It inhibits.","Not.","Increases.","Not."],"k":"↓ release = indirect sympatholytic.","rn":[["","Directly vs indirectly acting sympathomimetic","Directly vs indirectly acting sympathomimetic","Direct: adrenoceptor agonists; Indirect: alter release, metabolism, reuptake"]]},{"id":31103,"t":"Drugs and the ANS","s":"A patient’s constipation began after starting an asthma inhaler. Which drug class is most likely responsible?","o":["Muscarinic antagonist","β2 agonist, which speeds gut transit","β1 antagonist","AChE inhibitor","Muscarinic agonist"],"e":"Predict with rest and digest: a muscarinic antagonist stops the GI tract and bladder working – e.g. constipation, a massive adverse effect (anticholinergic).","w":["Not stated.","Not asthma.","Opposite.","Opposite."],"k":"Constipation: anticholinergic.","rn":[["","Asthma: how to decrease the parasympathetic?","Asthma: how to decrease the parasympathetic?","Muscarinic antagonist"],["","What is the muscarinic antagonist?","What is the muscarinic antagonist?","Atropine (belladonna)"],["","What does an anticholinergic drug do?","What does an anticholinergic drug do?","Decreases acetylcholine transmission; e.g. antagonist or blocker"]]},{"id":31104,"t":"Adrenergic Transmission","s":"Which statement about dopamine in the ANS is correct?","o":["It has little ANS role but matters in the CNS","It is the main postganglionic sympathetic transmitter in the periphery","It is only a precursor with no function","It acts at muscarinic receptors","It is an amino acid transmitter"],"e":"Dopamine does not play a big role in the ANS but is essential in the CNS for movement (Parkinson’s), emotion and reward. People used to think it was only a precursor of NA.","w":["NA.","Not anymore.","Dopamine receptors.","Monoamine."],"k":"Dopamine: CNS.","rn":[["","Does dopamine play a big role in the ANS?","Does dopamine play a big role in the ANS?","No; Essential in the CNS"],["","What 3 CNS roles does dopamine have?","What 3 CNS roles does dopamine have?","Movement (Parkinson's); Emotion; Reward pathways"]]},{"id":31105,"t":"Neurotransmitter Basics","s":"Which statement about the first antidepressants is correct?","o":["The first effective ones acted on noradrenaline","The first acted on serotonin","They acted on ACh","They acted on dopamine only","There were none"],"e":"First-line antidepressants now modulate serotonin, but the first ones with a real impact were drugs acting on noradrenaline.","w":["Serotonin drugs are first line now; the first acted on NA.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"First antidepressants: NA.","rn":[["","Which transmitter did the first effective antidepressants act on?","Which transmitter did the first effective antidepressants act on?","Noradrenaline; Positive effect on mood"]]},{"id":31106,"t":"Drugs and the ANS","s":"A selective β2 drug at a very high dose begins to affect the heart. Why?","o":["Subtypes are similar enough that β1 is bound","β2 receptors are converted into β1 receptors at high dose","β2 is Gαi","It blocks NET","It acts on nicotinic receptors"],"e":"Subtypes are structurally different but similar enough to be grouped: at too high a dose a β2 drug starts binding β1.","w":["Not.","Gαs.","Not.","Not."],"k":"High dose loses selectivity."},{"id":31107,"t":"Neurotransmitter Basics","s":"Which statement about where transmitters act relative to the synapse is correct?","o":["Also on presynaptic receptors if present","Only on postsynaptic receptors, never on the releasing neuron","Only in the blood","Only in the CNS","Only on glands"],"e":"On the other side are receptors on a postsynaptic neuron or effector cell. Transmitters can also act on receptors on the presynaptic neuron if there are any (e.g. α2).","w":["α2 is presynaptic.","Hormones.","Periphery too.","Not only."],"k":"Presynaptic receptors exist.","rn":[["","Besides the postsynaptic cell, where else can a neurotransmitter act?","Besides the postsynaptic cell, where else can a neurotransmitter act?","Receptors on the presynaptic neuron"],["","Where are α2 receptors found (synapse)?","Where are α2 receptors found (synapse)?","Postsynaptic; And a lot presynaptic"]]},{"id":31108,"t":"Neurotransmitter Basics","s":"Which statement about where adrenaline is synthesised is correct?","o":["Some transmitters, like adrenaline, are made only in vesicles","All are made only in the cytosol","Adrenaline is made in the nucleus","Adrenaline is made in the cleft","Adrenaline is made in the postsynaptic cell"],"e":"The transmitter is produced in the presynaptic neuron and goes into vesicles via specific transporters; some are synthesised only inside the vesicles, like adrenaline.","w":["Some, like adrenaline, are made in vesicles.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Adrenaline: made in vesicles.","rn":[["","What is a multi-transmitter neurone named after?","What is a multi-transmitter neurone named after?","The one made; In highest amount"]]},{"id":31109,"t":"Drugs and the ANS","s":"A drug blocks β1 receptors. Which effect is expected on the heart?","o":["Lower heart rate and force","Higher heart rate and force from reflex sympathetic drive","No effect on the heart","Bronchodilation","Increased salivation"],"e":"β1 on the heart increases rate and force; blocking β1 decreases heart rate and strength of contraction – lowering blood pressure.","w":["Not.","It acts.","β2.","Muscarinic."],"k":"β1 block: ↓ rate, force.","rn":[["","What does a β1 antagonist do to the heart?","What does a β1 antagonist do to the heart?","Lowers heart rate and force"]]},{"id":31110,"t":"Adrenergic Transmission","s":"The noradrenergic system is a drug target for which conditions?","o":["Hypertension, asthma, angina","Alzheimer’s, glaucoma and myasthenia","Diabetes only","Epilepsy only","None"],"e":"NA/Adr are most important in the sympathetic system; drugs that target any step of the life cycle treat hypertension, asthma, angina…","w":["Not listed.","Not.","Not.","Many."],"k":"NA drugs: hypertension, asthma, angina.","rn":[["","Drugs targeting the adrenergic life cycle treat which 3 conditions?","Drugs targeting the adrenergic life cycle treat which 3 conditions?","Hypertension; Asthma; Angina"]]},{"id":31111,"t":"Cholinergic Transmission","s":"Which pairing of muscarinic location and effect is correct?","o":["Bladder smooth muscle – contraction","Bladder smooth muscle – relaxation, preventing urination","Heart – increased rate","Airways – dilation","Salivary glands – decreased salivation"],"e":"Muscarinic: airway smooth muscle bronchoconstriction; heart decreased rate; gut contraction; salivary glands increased salivation; bladder smooth muscle contraction.","w":["β3.","Decreased.","Constriction.","Increased."],"k":"Muscarinic bladder: contraction.","rn":[["","What does muscarinic activation do in bladder smooth muscle?","What does muscarinic activation do in bladder smooth muscle?","Contraction"]]},{"id":31112,"t":"Neurotransmitter Basics","s":"A patient arrives with a full bladder and cannot pass urine. Thinking about drugs, what should you consider?","o":["An anticholinergic drug cause","A muscarinic agonist, which relaxes the bladder smooth muscle","A β1 antagonist","A NET inhibitor","An AChE inhibitor"],"e":"Urinary retention is an anticholinergic effect: a patient arriving with a full bladder who cannot go – think of a drug cause.","w":["It contracts the bladder.","Not.","Not stated.","Increases ACh."],"k":"Retention: think anticholinergic.","rn":[["","What does an anticholinergic drug do?","What does an anticholinergic drug do?","Decreases acetylcholine transmission; e.g. antagonist or blocker"]]},{"id":31113,"t":"Adrenergic Transmission","s":"Why may monoamine drugs affect other monoamine systems?","o":["Members of a class share chemistry, so overlap occurs","All monoamines share one receptor, so any drug acts on all of them","Monoamines are not related","They are ion channels","They are amino acids"],"e":"Members of a class share chemistry, so a drug for one monoamine system often has effects in other monoamine systems; even a receptor-selective drug may, at higher doses, start acting at other monoamine receptors.","w":["Different receptors.","Related.","GPCRs mostly.","Not."],"k":"Shared chemistry → overlap."},{"id":31114,"t":"Drugs and the ANS","s":"Which statement about using a nicotinic drug to slow the heart is correct?","o":["It would affect far more than the heart","It would be ideal, as nicotinic receptors are only in the heart","It is first line","Nicotinic receptors slow the heart via Gi","It has no effect"],"e":"A nicotinic drug would affect a lot more (ganglia of both divisions, NMJ, CNS) – muscarinic is the receptor mainly in the heart.","w":["Not.","Not.","Ion channel.","It would."],"k":"Nicotinic drug: too broad.","rn":[["","Too much β2 agonist in the blood affects which 2 things?","Too much β2 agonist in the blood affects which 2 things?","Potassium levels; Heart rate"]]},{"id":31115,"t":"Drugs and the ANS","s":"Which effect would a parasympatholytic drug have on the eye?","o":["Blurred vision","Sharper vision with constricted pupils and better accommodation","No effect","Excess tears","Tremor"],"e":"Anticholinergic (parasympatholytic) effects include blurred vision, dry mouth, urinary retention, constipation, tachycardia.","w":["Opposite.","Not.","Lacrimation is parasympathetic stimulation.","β2."],"k":"Anticholinergic: blurred vision.","rn":[["","Anticholinergic: 2 head ADRs?","Anticholinergic: 2 head ADRs?","Blurred vision; Dry mouth"]]},{"id":31116,"t":"Cholinergic Transmission","s":"Which receptor would a drug mimicking the effect of muscarine act on?","o":["Muscarinic receptors (GPCRs)","Nicotinic receptors (ion channels) at the ganglia","α1","β1","NET"],"e":"Muscarinic receptors are named after muscarine, the first compound found to act on them; agonists ACh and muscarine.","w":["Nicotine.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Muscarine → muscarinic.","rn":[["","Muscarinic vs nicotinic: receptor type","Muscarinic vs nicotinic: receptor type","Muscarinic: metabotropic (GPCR); Nicotinic: ionotropic (pentameric cation channel)"],["","What type of receptor are all adrenoceptors?","What type of receptor are all adrenoceptors?","Metabotropic (GPCR)"]]},{"id":31117,"t":"The ANS and Its Transmitters","s":"Which transmitter is released by preganglionic neurons in both divisions?","o":["ACh, acting on nicotinic receptors","NA in the sympathetic and ACh in the parasympathetic division","Adrenaline","Dopamine","ACh acting on muscarinic receptors"],"e":"Both preganglionic neurons release ACh onto nicotinic receptors on postganglionic neurons.","w":["Both ACh.","Not.","Not.","Nicotinic at ganglia."],"k":"Preganglionic: ACh → nicotinic.","rn":[["","What acts on the adrenal medulla?","What acts on the adrenal medulla?","Preganglionic neuron releasing ACh; On nicotinic receptors"],["","What do somatic efferent neurons release onto skeletal muscle?","What do somatic efferent neurons release onto skeletal muscle?","ACh; Acts on nicotinic receptors"]]},{"id":31118,"t":"Adrenergic Transmission","s":"Why is metabolism (MAO, COMT) described as of little importance in the periphery?","o":["Reuptake by NET is the main route there","MAO and COMT are absent from the body outside the brain","MAO metabolises only ACh","NA is not metabolised","Metabolism only occurs in blood"],"e":"The main mechanism of NA termination is reuptake via NET (majority). Metabolism by MAO/COMT has little importance in the periphery and more in the CNS.","w":["Not stated.","Monoamines.","It is.","Not."],"k":"Periphery: reuptake.","rn":[["","What is the main mechanism that ends NA action?","What is the main mechanism that ends NA action?","Reuptake by NAT (NET)"]]},{"id":31119,"t":"Neurotransmitter Basics","s":"Which best summarises how the two ANS divisions differ pharmacologically?","o":["Different postganglionic transmitters and receptors","Identical transmitters throughout","They act only via ion channels","They have no drug targets","They are voluntary systems"],"e":"They are pharmacologically distinct (for the most part), working through different transmitters and receptor types: parasympathetic ACh → muscarinic; sympathetic NA → adrenoceptors (exceptions: adrenal medulla, sweat glands).","w":["They differ at the postganglionic level.","Metabotropic mostly.","Many.","Involuntary."],"k":"Divisions: distinct transmitters.","rn":[["","Noradrenaline and adrenaline: same or different receptors?","Noradrenaline and adrenaline: same or different receptors?","Same receptors; Adrenoceptors"],["","Image card: Which ANS division supplies this target? Why is it unusual?","Which ANS division supplies this target? Why is it unusual?","Sympathetic; Postganglionic is cholinergic; ACh on muscarinic receptors"],["","Why is the sweat gland a special case?","Why is the sweat gland a special case?","Postganglionic is cholinergic; ACh on muscarinic receptors"]]},{"id":31120,"t":"Drugs and the ANS","s":"A drug causes bronchodilation, tachycardia and tremor. Which receptor action best explains all three?","o":["β agonism (β2, with β1 at high levels)","Muscarinic agonism in the airways, heart and muscle","α1 antagonism","Nicotinic antagonism","AChE inhibition"],"e":"β2 agonism: bronchodilation and tremor (skeletal muscle); tachycardia occurs with too much β2 agonist (β1 effects). This matches salbutamol’s ADR profile.","w":["Opposite.","Not.","Not.","Parasympathetic."],"k":"β2 agonist profile."},{"id":31121,"t":"Drugs and the ANS","s":"A 55-year-old man with hypertension and asthma is started on propranolol. His blood pressure falls, but within days he becomes wheezy, his heart rate is slow, his hands feel cold and he feels tired. His GP switches him to bisoprolol and his breathing improves.<br><br>Why did propranolol cause wheeze, and why is bisoprolol better?","o":["Propranolol also blocks bronchial β2; bisoprolol is β1-selective","Propranolol activates muscarinic receptors; bisoprolol blocks them","Propranolol is an α antagonist; bisoprolol is a β2 agonist","Propranolol blocks nicotinic receptors at the ganglia; bisoprolol only acts on the CNS","Bisoprolol is a non-selective β-blocker that dilates the bronchi through β1 receptors"],"e":"Step 1: β1 receptors in the heart raise heart rate and force, while β2 receptors in the bronchi cause dilation; a non-selective β antagonist like propranolol blocks both, giving bronchoconstriction, bradycardia, cold extremities and fatigue. Step 2: β1-selective antagonists (atenolol, bisoprolol) lower blood pressure with fewer side effects such as bronchoconstriction, although selectivity depends on dose.","w":["Propranolol is a β antagonist, not a muscarinic agonist.","Propranolol is a β, not α, antagonist, and bisoprolol is a blocker.","Propranolol acts on β-adrenoceptors.","Bisoprolol is β1-selective, and β1 is in the heart."],"k":"Propranolol blocks β1+β2 (bronchospasm); bisoprolol β1-selective.","rn":[["","Why is β1 selective better than propranolol?","Why is β1 selective better than propranolol?","Fewer side effects; e.g. less bronchoconstriction"]]},{"id":31122,"t":"Drugs and the ANS","s":"A 70-year-old woman with chronic lung disease uses an inhaled muscarinic antagonist to open her airways. After her dose is increased, she complains of blurred vision, a dry mouth, constipation and difficulty passing urine, and her heart rate is fast.<br><br>Which receptors does the drug block, and why do these effects occur?","o":["Muscarinic receptors; blocking parasympathetic effects in eye, glands, gut, bladder and heart","β2 receptors; blocking sympathetic bronchodilation","Nicotinic receptors at the motor endplate; causing muscle weakness","α1 receptors; causing vasodilation","Muscarinic receptors; increasing salivation and slowing the heart through M2 activation"],"e":"Step 1: muscarinic antagonists (e.g. ipratropium) block parasympathetic bronchoconstriction, which helps the airways. Step 2: blocking muscarinic receptors elsewhere gives the well-known anticholinergic effects: blurred vision, dry mouth, urinary retention, constipation and tachycardia, because parasympathetic actions such as salivation, gut contraction, bladder contraction and slowing of the heart are lost.","w":["The drug is a muscarinic antagonist, not a β2 blocker.","Nicotinic blockade at the endplate is not described.","α1 blockade is not the drug's action.","Blocking muscarinic receptors reduces salivation and speeds the heart."],"k":"Muscarinic antagonists → anticholinergic effects (dry mouth, retention, tachycardia).","rn":[["","Hypertension: muscarinic or nicotinic agonist?","Hypertension: muscarinic or nicotinic agonist?","Muscarinic; Receptor in the heart"],["","Why is the sweat gland a special case?","Why is the sweat gland a special case?","Postganglionic is cholinergic; ACh on muscarinic receptors"],["","Where are muscarinic receptors located?","Where are muscarinic receptors located?","Postsynaptically; Smooth muscle, cardiac muscle, glands"]]},{"id":31123,"t":"Drugs and the ANS","s":"A 20-year-old with asthma takes high-dose salbutamol tablets for a few days instead of using her inhaler. Her wheeze improves but she develops a tremor of her hands and a fast heart rate. Her doctor advises returning to the inhaler.<br><br>Why do these side effects occur, and why is the inhaled route better?","o":["β2 activation in muscle causes tremor; inhalation limits systemic effects","Muscarinic activation causes tremor; inhalation increases systemic levels","α1 blockade causes tremor; inhalation blocks α1 receptors","Salbutamol is a β1 antagonist; inhalation makes it β2-selective","Salbutamol blocks nicotinic receptors at the motor endplate, so inhalation avoids the skeletal muscles"],"e":"Step 1: salbutamol is a β2 agonist, first-line for asthma because β2 agonism causes bronchodilation; β2 receptors in skeletal muscle mediate tremor, and adverse effects also include increased blood flow to skeletal muscle and tachycardia. Step 2: giving β2 agonists by inhalation limits these adverse effects by delivering the drug mainly to the airways.","w":["Salbutamol is a β2 agonist, and inhalation reduces systemic levels.","Salbutamol does not act on α1 receptors.","Salbutamol is a β2 agonist, not a β1 antagonist.","Salbutamol does not act on nicotinic receptors."],"k":"Salbutamol (β2 agonist): tremor, tachycardia; inhalation limits ADRs.","rn":[["","What are the 3 β2 agonist ADRs?","What are the 3 β2 agonist ADRs?","Tremor; More muscle blood flow; Tachycardia"],["","How are β2 agonist ADRs kept very limited?","How are β2 agonist ADRs kept very limited?","Inhalation"],["","What does β2 do in skeletal muscle?","What does β2 do in skeletal muscle?","Tremor; Glycogenolysis"]]},{"id":31124,"t":"The ANS and Its Transmitters","s":"A neuroscientist traces two autonomic pathways to the heart. Pathway 1 starts in the brainstem, has a long preganglionic fibre and a short postganglionic fibre near the heart, and slows the heart. Pathway 2 starts at T1–L2, has a short preganglionic and long postganglionic fibre, and speeds the heart.<br><br>Which transmitters and receptors are used at the target organ by each pathway?","o":["1: ACh on muscarinic receptors; 2: noradrenaline on adrenoceptors","1: noradrenaline on adrenoceptors; 2: ACh on muscarinic receptors","Both: ACh on nicotinic receptors at the heart","1: adrenaline from the adrenal medulla; 2: dopamine on D receptors","1: ACh on nicotinic receptors; 2: ACh on muscarinic receptors, as in sweat glands"],"e":"Step 1: pathway 1 is parasympathetic (cranio-sacral, long pre- and short postganglionic), and pathway 2 is sympathetic (thoracolumbar T1–L2, short pre- and long postganglionic); in both, preganglionic neurons release ACh onto nicotinic receptors. Step 2: parasympathetic postganglionic neurons release ACh onto muscarinic receptors, while sympathetic postganglionic neurons release noradrenaline onto adrenoceptors.","w":["The transmitters are swapped.","Nicotinic receptors are at the ganglia, not the heart.","Dopamine does not play a big role in the ANS.","The heart uses muscarinic receptors parasympathetically and adrenoceptors sympathetically."],"k":"Postganglionic: parasympathetic ACh → muscarinic; sympathetic NA → adrenoceptors.","rn":[["","Image card: Which ANS division supplies this target? Why is it unusual?","Which ANS division supplies this target? Why is it unusual?","Sympathetic; Postganglionic is cholinergic; ACh on muscarinic receptors"],["","Why is the sweat gland a special case?","Why is the sweat gland a special case?","Postganglionic is cholinergic; ACh on muscarinic receptors"],["","Noradrenaline and adrenaline: same or different receptors?","Noradrenaline and adrenaline: same or different receptors?","Same receptors; Adrenoceptors"]]},{"id":31125,"t":"The ANS and Its Transmitters","s":"A patient given a muscarinic antagonist stops sweating normally, even though sweating is a sympathetic response. Another patient has a tumour of the adrenal medulla, which releases large amounts of hormone directly into the blood, causing a racing heart and high blood pressure.<br><br>Which statement explains both observations?","o":["Sweat gland neurones are cholinergic; chromaffin cells release mainly adrenaline","Sweat glands use nicotinic receptors; the adrenal medulla releases ACh","Sweat glands use β3 receptors; the adrenal medulla is parasympathetic","Sweat glands are parasympathetic; the adrenal medulla releases dopamine","Sweat glands use α2 receptors; the adrenal medulla releases noradrenaline only, through long postganglionic axons"],"e":"Step 1: in the special case of sweat glands, sympathetic postganglionic neurones are cholinergic, so their effects are mediated by muscarinic receptors and blocked by muscarinic antagonists. Step 2: in the adrenal medulla, postganglionic axons never develop; chromaffin cells take on an endocrine role and release adrenaline and noradrenaline (about 80% adrenaline) into the bloodstream.","w":["Sweat glands use muscarinic receptors, and the medulla releases catecholamines.","Sweating is sympathetic via muscarinic receptors.","Sweat glands are sympathetically supplied.","The medulla has no postganglionic axons and releases mainly adrenaline."],"k":"Sweat glands: sympathetic but muscarinic; adrenal medulla: ~80% adrenaline into blood.","rn":[["","Image card: Why is this a special case of sympathetic supply? Which cells release the hormone?","Why is this a special case of sympathetic supply? Which cells release the hormone?","No postganglionic neuron; Chromaffin cells; About 80% adrenaline (+ noradrenaline)"],["","What do chromaffin cells release into the bloodstream?","What do chromaffin cells release into the bloodstream?","Adrenaline and noradrenaline; About 80% adrenaline"],["","What mainly secretes adrenaline?","What mainly secretes adrenaline?","Chromaffin cells; Adrenal medulla"]]},{"id":31126,"t":"Adrenergic Transmission","s":"A researcher activates three adrenoceptor types in different cells. Receptor X raises IP3 and DAG and causes vascular smooth muscle contraction. Receptor Y on presynaptic sympathetic neurones lowers cAMP and reduces noradrenaline release. Receptor Z raises cAMP and increases heart rate.<br><br>Which receptors are X, Y and Z?","o":["X: α1 (Gq); Y: α2 (Gi); Z: β1 (Gs)","X: β1 (Gs); Y: α1 (Gq); Z: α2 (Gi)","X: α2 (Gi); Y: β2 (Gs); Z: α1 (Gq)","X: M3 (Gq); Y: M2 (Gi); Z: nicotinic","X: α1 (Gs); Y: α2 (Gq); Z: β1 (Gi), as β receptors lower cAMP"],"e":"Step 1: α1 adrenoceptors couple to Gαq (phospholipase C, IP3 and DAG) and cause vasoconstriction, and presynaptic α2 adrenoceptors couple to Gαi (adenylyl cyclase inhibited) and decrease noradrenaline release (negative feedback). Step 2: β adrenoceptors couple to Gαs (adenylyl cyclase activated, cAMP), and β1 in the heart increases rate and force.","w":["The receptors are mismatched.","α2 is Gi and presynaptic; β2 is in bronchi/vessels.","These are acetylcholine receptors, not adrenoceptors.","α1 is Gq, α2 is Gi and β is Gs."],"k":"α1 Gq (vasoconstriction); α2 Gi (↓NA release); β Gs (↑cAMP).","rn":[["","How does α2 decrease NA release?","How does α2 decrease NA release?","Gαi lowers cAMP; Stops calcium entry; Less vesicle release"],["","Image card: When NA binds this presynaptic receptor, what happens inside the neuron and what is the…","When NA binds this presynaptic receptor, what happens inside the neuron and what is the end result?","Less cAMP and Ca2+ entry; Less NA release (negative feedback)"]]},{"id":31127,"t":"Adrenergic Transmission","s":"A drug blocks the transporter in the presynaptic membrane that normally takes noradrenaline back into sympathetic nerve terminals. Another drug inhibits the enzyme that breaks down acetylcholine in the synaptic cleft.<br><br>What is the effect of each drug on its transmitter?","o":["Both prolong action: NET reuptake and AChE breakdown are blocked","Both shorten transmitter action","The first speeds NA breakdown by MAO; the second speeds ACh release","The first blocks adrenoceptors; the second blocks nicotinic receptors","Both have no effect, because NA and ACh are mostly removed by COMT and MAO in the periphery"],"e":"Step 1: noradrenaline's action is terminated mainly by reuptake via the noradrenaline transporter (NAT/NET) into the presynaptic neuron, with MAO and COMT of little importance in the periphery, so blocking NET prolongs NA's action. Step 2: acetylcholine is terminated by metabolism by cholinesterases (AChE and BuChE), so an AChE inhibitor (e.g. neostigmine) prolongs ACh's action.","w":["Blocking termination prolongs action.","The drugs block termination, not speed it.","They act on termination, not receptors.","Reuptake (NET) is the main route for NA in the periphery."],"k":"NA ended mainly by NET reuptake; ACh by AChE/BuChE.","rn":[["","What is the main mechanism that ends NA action?","What is the main mechanism that ends NA action?","Reuptake by NAT (NET)"]]},{"id":31128,"t":"Drugs and the ANS","s":"A doctor considers treating high blood pressure by stimulating the parasympathetic system with an acetylcholinesterase inhibitor such as neostigmine, which would slow the heart. A senior colleague advises against this because of the side effects.<br><br>Which adverse effects would be expected, and why?","o":["Bronchoconstriction, lacrimation, salivation and visual disturbance","Tremor and tachycardia from β2 stimulation","Dry mouth and urinary retention from muscarinic blockade","Postural hypotension and reflex tachycardia from α blockade","Bronchodilation and constipation, because the parasympathetic system relaxes airways and slows the gut"],"e":"Step 1: inhibiting acetylcholinesterase prevents ACh metabolism and stimulates the parasympathetic system, which would slow the heart. Step 2: but it would also stimulate parasympathetic effects everywhere: bronchoconstriction, lacrimation, salivation, visual disturbances and possibly incontinence (\"doesn't sound good!\").","w":["These are β2 agonist side effects.","These are anticholinergic (muscarinic antagonist) effects, the opposite.","These are α-antagonist side effects.","The parasympathetic system constricts airways and speeds the gut."],"k":"Parasympathetic stimulation: bronchoconstriction, salivation, lacrimation, vision, incontinence.","rn":[["","Parasympathetic stimulation for hypertension: what are the 4 ADRs?","Parasympathetic stimulation for hypertension: what are the 4 ADRs?","Bronchoconstriction; Lacrimation, salivation; Visual disturbances; Incontinence"]]},{"id":31129,"t":"Drugs and the ANS","s":"A patient is given phentolamine to lower his blood pressure. His peripheral resistance falls, but when he stands up he feels faint, he gets a headache and his heart races. His heart itself is not directly affected by the drug.<br><br>Which receptor does phentolamine block, and why do these effects occur?","o":["α-adrenoceptors on blood vessels; postural hypotension, headache and reflex tachycardia","β1 receptors in the heart; bradycardia","Muscarinic receptors; anticholinergic effects","β2 receptors in bronchi; bronchoconstriction","Nicotinic receptors at autonomic ganglia, blocking both divisions at once"],"e":"Step 1: phentolamine is an α antagonist; blocking α adrenoceptors on blood vessels could decrease peripheral resistance and blood pressure (not sustained), with no direct effect on the heart. Step 2: possible adverse effects include postural hypotension, headache and reflex tachycardia.","w":["Phentolamine has no direct effect on the heart.","Phentolamine is not a muscarinic antagonist.","Phentolamine does not act on β2 receptors.","Phentolamine acts on α-adrenoceptors."],"k":"Phentolamine (α antagonist): ↓ resistance; postural hypotension, reflex tachycardia.","rn":[["","What are the 3 ADRs of α antagonists?","What are the 3 ADRs of α antagonists?","Postural hypotension; Headache; Reflex tachycardia"]]},{"id":31130,"t":"Cholinergic Transmission","s":"A researcher stimulates muscarinic receptors in three tissues. In the heart, the receptor lowers cAMP and raises potassium conductance, slowing the rate. In salivary glands and smooth muscle, the receptor raises IP3, DAG and calcium. Nicotinic receptors at autonomic ganglia instead open directly as ion channels.<br><br>Which subtypes are in the heart and the glands?","o":["Heart: M2 (Gi); glands/smooth muscle: M3 (Gq)","Heart: M3 (Gq); glands: M2 (Gi)","Heart: M1 (Gs); glands: M4 (Gi)","Both nicotinic ion channels","Heart: β1 (Gs); glands: α1 (Gq), as muscarinic receptors are not found outside the CNS"],"e":"Step 1: the M2 (\"cardiac\") receptor couples to Gαi, inhibiting adenylyl cyclase and increasing potassium conductance, which decreases heart rate. Step 2: the M3 (\"glandular/smooth muscle\") receptor couples to Gαq, activating phospholipase C to raise IP3, DAG and Ca²⁺; nicotinic receptors are ionotropic cation channels.","w":["The subtypes are swapped.","M1 is \"neural\" and Gq; M4 and M5 are CNS.","Muscarinic receptors are GPCRs, not ion channels.","Muscarinic receptors are found peripherally in heart, glands and smooth muscle."],"k":"M2 (heart, Gi, ↑gK⁺); M3 (glands/smooth muscle, Gq).","rn":[["","Where are muscarinic receptors located?","Where are muscarinic receptors located?","Postsynaptically; Smooth muscle, cardiac muscle, glands"],["","M1 vs M2 vs M3: G protein","M1 vs M2 vs M3: G protein","M1 (neural): Gαq; M2 (cardiac): Gαi; M3 (glandular/smooth muscle): Gαq"],["","Which effector organs does the parasympathetic supply?","Which effector organs does the parasympathetic supply?","Smooth muscle; Cardiac muscle; Glands"]]},{"id":31501,"t":"Adrenergic Transmission","s":"At a noradrenergic synapse, transporter <b>8</b> returns released transmitter into the presynaptic terminal and is blocked by cocaine. Name it.","a":["net","noradrenaline transporter","norepinephrine transporter","uptake 1","reuptake transporter"],"e":"8 is the reuptake transporter (NET for noradrenaline), the main peripheral termination route.","k":"8 = NET.","img":{"src":"31/o_synapse","cap":"Chemical synapse; A presynaptic, B postsynaptic. Image: Mouagip, CC BY-SA 3.0, Wikimedia Commons."},"rn":[["","What else do antidepressants and cocaine inhibit besides NET (noradrenaline transporter)?","What else do antidepressants and cocaine inhibit besides NET (noradrenaline transporter)?","Other monoamine transporters"],["","What does NAT (NET) stand for?","What does NAT (NET) stand for?","Noradrenaline (norepinephrine) transporter"],["","What 2 drug groups inhibit NET?","What 2 drug groups inhibit NET?","Antidepressants; Cocaine"]]},{"id":31502,"t":"Adrenergic Transmission","s":"Receptor <b>3</b> sits on the presynaptic terminal and reduces further noradrenaline release when activated. Which adrenoceptor subtype plays this role?","a":["alpha2","α2","alpha 2","alpha-2","a2"],"e":"3 is a presynaptic autoreceptor; α2 (Gαi) lowers cAMP and Ca²⁺ entry, reducing NA release.","k":"3 = α2 autoreceptor.","img":{"src":"31/o_synapse","cap":"Chemical synapse; A presynaptic, B postsynaptic. Image: Mouagip, CC BY-SA 3.0, Wikimedia Commons."}},{"id":31503,"t":"Cholinergic Transmission","s":"A drug blocks transporter <b>8</b> at peripheral noradrenergic synapses. What would you predict?","o":["More noradrenaline stays in the cleft, raising heart rate","Less noradrenaline is released, slowing the heart","Noradrenaline is broken down faster by MAO in the cleft","Postsynaptic receptors 5 are destroyed","Vesicles 2 can no longer fuse"],"w":["Release is not blocked; removal is.","MAO/COMT are minor in the periphery.","Receptors are not destroyed.","Fusion depends on Ca²⁺ via channel 6."],"e":"Blocking reuptake (NET), as cocaine does, leaves NA in the cleft: pounding heartbeat. It is an indirectly-acting sympathomimetic effect.","k":"Block NET → more NA.","img":{"src":"31/o_synapse","cap":"Chemical synapse; A presynaptic, B postsynaptic. Image: Mouagip, CC BY-SA 3.0, Wikimedia Commons."},"rn":[["","What does muscarinic activation do in the heart?","What does muscarinic activation do in the heart?","Decreased heart rate"],["","What does a β1 antagonist do to the heart?","What does a β1 antagonist do to the heart?","Lowers heart rate and force"],["","What does inhibiting the sympathetic decrease?","What does inhibiting the sympathetic decrease?","Heart rate; Strength of contraction; Peripheral resistance"]]}]);
